== 2. The Main Objections Against Closet Prayer Answered

=== Objection 1: We Have Much Business upon Our Hands

But many will be ready to object and say, “We have much business on our hands, and we cannot spare time for private prayer. We have so much to do in our shops, and in our warehouses, and out among others, that we cannot spare time to wait on the Lord in our closets.”

Now to this objection I will give these eight answers, so that this objection may never rise again in any of your#super[+] hearts.

==== 1. What Is Your Business Compared to That of Abraham, Moses, David, and Daniel?

First, what are all the business matters on your#super[+] hands compared to the business and weighty affairs that lay on the hands of Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, Moses, David, Daniel, Elijah, Nehemiah, Peter, and Cornelius? And yet you#super[+] find all these worthies exercising themselves in private prayer. And the king is commanded to read some part of God’s Word every day, notwithstanding all his great and weighty duties (Deut 17:18–20). Now certainly, sirs, your#super[+] great business affairs are little more than ciphers compared with theirs. If there were any on earth who might have pleaded an exemption from private prayer on account of business, of much business, of great business, these might have done it; but they were too honest and too noble to neglect so choice a duty on account of much business. These noble hearts made all their public duties bow to private prayer; they would never allow their public duties to tread private prayer underfoot.

==== 2. None Ever Prospered More Than Those Who Devoted Themselves to Private Prayer

Secondly, I answer that no men’s outward affairs ever prospered more than the affairs of those who devoted themselves to private prayer, notwithstanding their many and great worldly duties. Witness the prosperity and outward flourishing condition of Moses, Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, Nehemiah, David, Daniel, and Cornelius. These were much with God in their closets, and God blessed their blessings to them (Gen 22:17). How their cups overflowed! What remarkable favors God heaped on them and theirs! No families have been so prospered, protected, and graced as the families of those who have maintained secret communion with God in a corner (1 Chr 11:9).

Private prayer best speeds our temporal affairs. He who prays well in his closet will be sure to fare well in his shop, or at his plow, or in whatever else he turns his hand to (1 Tim 4:8). It is true, Abimelech was rich as well as Abraham, and Laban was rich as well as Jacob, and Saul was a king as well as David, and Julian was an emperor as well as Constantine; but it was only Abraham, Jacob, David, and Constantine who had their blessings blessed to them. All the rest had their blessings cursed to them (Prov 3:33; Mal 2:2). They had many good things, but they did not have “the favor of Him who dwelt in the burning bush” with what they had (Deut 33:16); and therefore all their blessings were only bitter-sweets to them. Though all the sons of Jacob returned from Egypt laden with grain, with money in their sacks, yet only Benjamin had the silver cup in the mouth of his sack. So though the men of the world have their grain and their money, etc., yet it is only God’s Benjamins who have the silver cup, the grace cup, “the cup of blessing,” as the apostle calls it, for their portion (1 Cor 10:16). Oh, sirs, as ever you#super[+] would prosper and flourish in the world, as ever you would have your water turned into wine, your temporal blessings into spiritual benefits, be much with God in your closets.

==== 3. You Fool Away an Hour a Day, and Yet Plead the Lack of Time

Thirdly, I answer: it is ten to one that the objector every day fools away, or trifles away, or idles away, or sins away, one hour of the day; why then should he plead the lack of time? There are none who toil and moil and busy themselves most in their worldly employments who do not spend an hour or more a day to little or no purpose, either in gazing about, or in dallying, or toying, or courting, or in telling stories, or in busying themselves in other men’s affairs, or in idle visits, or in smoking a pipe, etc. Why then should not these men redeem an hour’s time a day for private prayer out of the time which they usually spend so vainly and idly? Can you#super[+], notwithstanding all your great worldly employments, find an hour in the day to catch flies in, as Domitian the emperor did, and to play the fool in? And can you#super[+] not find an hour in the day to wait on God in your closets?

There were three special faults of which Cato declared he had seriously repented: one was going by water when he might have gone by land; another was trusting a secret to a woman’s bosom; but the chief was spending an hour unprofitably. This heathen will one day rise up in judgment against those who, notwithstanding their great employments, spend many hours a week unprofitably, and yet cry out with the Duke of Alva that they have so much to do on earth that they have no time to look up to heaven. It was a base and sordid spirit in King Sardanapalus, who spent much of his time among women, spinning and carding wool, time which should have been spent in ruling and governing his kingdom. So it is a base, sordid spirit in anyone to spend any of his time in toying and trifling, and then to cry out that he has so much business to do in the world that he has no time for closet prayer, no time to serve God, nor to save his own precious and immortal soul.

==== 4. No Man Dares Plead This Before the Lord Jesus in the Great Day of Account

Fourthly, I answer: no man dares plead this objection before the Lord Jesus in the great day of account (Eccl 11:9; Rom 14:10; 2 Cor 5:10). Why then should any man be so childish and foolish, so ignorant and impudent, as to plead before men what cannot be pleaded before the judgment seat of Christ? Oh, sirs, as you#super[+] love your souls, and as you would be happy forever, never fob off your own consciences, or others’, with any pleas, arguments, or objections now that you dare not acknowledge and stand by when you lie on a dying bed, and when you appear before the whole court of heaven, etc. In the great day of account, when the secrets of all hearts will be made manifest, and God will call men to a reckoning before angels, men, and devils for the neglect of private prayer, all guilty persons will be found speechless. Not a man or woman will be found who will dare to stand up and say, “Lord, I would have waited on You in my closet, but I had so much business to do in the world that I had no time to enjoy secret communion with You in a corner.” It is the greatest wisdom in the world to plead nothing by way of excuse in this our day that we dare not plead in the great day.

==== 5. It Is Our Duty to Redeem Time from All Our Secular Business

Fifthly, I answer that it is our duty to redeem time from all our secular business for private prayer. All sorts of Christians, whether slave or free, rich or poor, high or low, superiors or inferiors, are expressly charged by God to redeem time for prayer, for private prayer, as well as for other holy exercises. “Devote yourselves#super[+] to prayer, being watchful and thankful, as you pray also for us, that God may open to us a door for the word, so that we may proclaim the mystery of Christ, for which I am in chains” (Col 4:2–3).

But here some may object and say, “We have so much business to do in the world that we have no time for prayer.” The apostle answers this objection in verse 5: “Act wisely toward outsiders, redeeming the time.” “Redeeming the time, because the days are evil” (Eph 5:16)—ἐξαγοραζόμενοι τὸν καιρόν, that is, buying up, gaining the time. The words are a metaphor taken from merchants who prefer the least profit that may be gained to their pleasures or delights, closely following their business while the markets are at their best. A merchant, when he comes to a market or fair, takes the first season and opportunity of buying his goods. He does not put it off, at the risk of the evening or the next morning, in hope of a better bargain, but he makes use of the present season and buys before the market is over.

Others take the words this way: “Purchase at any cost all occasions and opportunities of doing good, so that you#super[+] may thereby in some measure redeem that precious jewel of time which you have lost in the past.” They are like travelers who have loitered on the way or stayed long at their inn: when they find night coming on, they quicken their pace and go as many miles in an hour as they did before in many. Though time let slip is in itself beyond recovery, yet in a moral sense it is counted as regained when men double their care, diligence, and efforts to redeem it. The best Christian is he who is the greatest monopolizer of time for private prayer. There is no Christian like him who redeems time from his worldly affairs and his lawful comforts and recreations to be with God in his closet. David, having tasted the sweetness, goodness, and graciousness of God, cannot stay in his bed, but will borrow some time from his sleep, so that he might take some turns in Paradise and pour out his soul in prayer and praises when no eye was open to see him, nor any ear open to hear him, but all were asleep around him (Ps 63:6). “At midnight I rise to give You thanks … I rise before dawn and cry for help” (Ps 119:62, 147). David was up and at private prayer before daybreak. David was no sluggish Christian, no slothful Christian, no lazy Christian. He used to be in his closet when others were sleeping in their beds. “My eyes anticipate the watches of night, that I may meditate on Your word … My soul waits for the Lord more than watchmen wait for the morning—more than watchmen wait for the morning” (Ps 119:148; 130:6). Just as the weary sentinel on a dark, cold, wet night waits and peeps, and peeps and waits, for the appearance of the morning, so David waited and peeped, and peeped and waited, for the first and fittest season to pour out his soul before God in a corner. David would never allow his worldly business to crowd out holy exercises. He would often borrow time from the world for private prayer, but he would never borrow time from private prayer to spend it on the world.

Mr. Bradford, the martyr, counted that hour lost in which he did not do some good, with his pen, his tongue, or his purse.

Ignatius, when he heard a clock strike, used to say, “Now I have one hour more to answer for.”

So the early Christians would redeem some time from their sleep so that they might be with God in their closets, as Clement observes.

And I have read of Theodosius the emperor that, after the various worldly employments relating to his civil affairs in the daytime were over, he used to consecrate the greatest part of the night to the study of the Scriptures and private prayer. For this purpose he had a lamp so skillfully made that it supplied itself with oil, so that he might in no way be interrupted in his private retreats.

That time ought to be redeemed is a lesson that has been taught by the very heathen themselves. It was the saying of Pittacus, one of the seven wise men, “Know time; lose not a minute.” And so Theophrastus used to say, “Time is of precious cost.” And so Seneca: “Time is the only thing,” he says, “of which we can innocently be covetous; and yet there is nothing of which many are more lavishly and extravagantly wasteful.” And Chrestus, a sophist of Byzantium in the time of the emperor Hadrian, was much given to wine; yet he always counted time so precious that when he had misspent his time all day, he would redeem it at night.

When Titus Vespasian, who avenged Christ’s blood on Jerusalem, returned to Rome as victor, he remembered one night, as he sat at supper with his friends, that he had done no good that day, and he uttered this memorable and praiseworthy saying: “#emph[Amici, diem perdidi]” (My friends, I have lost a day).

Chilo, one of the seven sages, being asked what was the hardest thing in the world to do, answered, “To use and employ a man’s time well.”

Cato held that an account must be given not only of our labor but also of our leisure. And Aelian gives this testimony of the Spartans, that “they were hugely covetous of their time, spending it all on necessary things, and allowing no citizen either to be idle or to play.” And, says another, “We trifle with what is most precious, and throw away what it is most in our interest to redeem.”

Certainly these heathen will rise in judgment, not only against Domitian the Roman emperor, who spent much of his time in killing flies; nor only against Archimedes, who spent his time drawing lines on the ground when Syracuse was taken; nor against Artaxerxes, who spent his time making handles for knives; nor only against Suleiman the Great Turk, who spent his time making notches of horn for bows; nor only against Aeropus, a Macedonian king, who spent his time making lanterns; nor only against Hyrcanus the king of Parthia, who spent his time catching moles; but also against many professing Christians who, instead of redeeming precious time, trifle and fool away much of their precious time at the mirror, the comb, the lute, the viol, the pipe, or at vain sports and foolish pastimes, or by idle jesting, immoderate sleeping, and superfluous feasting, etc. Oh, sirs! Good hours and blessed opportunities for closet prayer are merchandise of the highest rate and price; and therefore whoever has a mind to be rich in grace and to be high in glory should buy up that merchandise. They should always be redeeming precious time. Oh, sirs! We should redeem time for private prayer out of our eating time, our drinking time, our sleeping time, our buying time, our selling time, our sinning time, our sporting time, rather than neglect our closet communion with God!

==== 6. Closet Prayer Is Either a Duty or It Is No Duty

Sixthly, I answer: closet prayer is either a duty or it is no duty. Now I have proved so strongly, I suppose, that it is a duty, that no man or devil can fairly or honestly deny it to be a duty. Why then do men cry out about their great business? Alas! Duty must be done, whatever business is left undone. Duty must be done, or the man who neglects it will be undone forever. It is a vain thing to plead business when a required duty is to be performed. Indeed, if the bare pleading of business, of much business, were enough to excuse men from duty, I am afraid there are very few duties of the gospel that men would not try to evade under a pretense of business, of much business. He who pleads business to evade private prayer will be just as ready to plead business to evade family prayer; and he who pleads business to evade family prayer will be just as ready to plead business to evade public prayer.

Well, sirs, remember what became of those who excused themselves out of heaven by their carnal excuses and secular business. “I have bought a field, and I need to go see it. Please excuse me,” says one. “I have bought,” says another, “five yoke of oxen, and I am going to try them out. Please excuse me.” And, “I have married a wife,” says another, “so I cannot come” (Luke 14:18–20). The true reason why they would not come to the supper to which the King of kings had invited them was not that they had bought farms and oxen, but that their farms and oxen had bought them. The things of the world and their carnal relations had taken up so much room in their hearts and affections that they had no appetite for heaven’s dainties. Therefore it is worth noting what Christ adds at the end of the parable: whoever “does not hate his father and mother and wife and children and brothers and sisters—yes, even his own life,” and much more his farm and oxen, “cannot be My disciple” (v. 26).

By these words it is evident that it was not simply the farm, nor the oxen, nor the wife, but a foolish, inordinate, carnal love and esteem of these things, above better and greater blessings, that made them refuse the gracious invitation of Christ. They refused the grace and mercy of God offered in the gospel under a pretense of worldly business; and God decisively concludes that not one man of them will taste His supper. And indeed, what can be more just and righteous than that those should never so much as taste spiritual and eternal blessings who prefer their earthly business to heaven’s dainties, who, with the Reubenites, prefer a country suitable for feeding their cattle to a share in the land of promise?

Private prayer is a work of absolute necessity, both for bringing the heart into a good frame and for keeping the heart in a good frame. It is of absolute necessity for the revealing of sin, and for the preventing of sin, and for the embittering of sin, and for the weakening of sin, and for the purging away of sin. It is of absolute necessity for the revealing of grace, and for a full exercise of grace, and for an eminent increase of grace. It is of absolute necessity to arm us against both inward and outward temptations, afflictions, and sufferings. It is of absolute necessity to fit us for all other duties and services. For a man to glorify God, to save his own soul, and to further his own everlasting happiness is a work of the greatest necessity. Now private prayer is such a work; why then should any man plead business, great business, when a work of such absolute necessity is before him? If a man’s child or wife were dangerously sick, or wounded, or near death, he would never plead, “I have business, I have a great deal of business to do; and therefore I cannot stay with my child, my wife, and I have no time to go or send for the physician.” Oh, no! He would rather argue this way: “It is absolutely necessary that I look after the preservation of the life of my child or my wife, and I will attend to this whatever becomes of my business.” Oh, sirs! Your#super[+] souls are of greater concern to you than the lives of all the wives and children in the world; and therefore they must be attended to, they must be saved, whatever business is neglected.

==== 7. God Never Appointed Your Calling to Thrust Private Prayer Out of Doors

Seventhly, I answer that God never appointed or intended any man’s ordinary, particular calling to thrust private prayer out of doors. That it is a great sin for any professing Christian to neglect his particular calling under any religious pretense is evident enough from these scriptures: “Six days you shall labor and do all your work … Each one should remain in the calling he was in when he was called … For even while we were with you#super[+], we gave you this command: ‘If anyone is unwilling to work, he shall not eat.’ Yet we hear that some of you#super[+] are leading undisciplined lives and accomplishing nothing but being busybodies. We command and urge such people by our Lord Jesus Christ to begin working quietly to earn their own living … to aspire to live quietly, to attend to your#super[+] own matters, and to work with your own hands, as we instructed you. Then you#super[+] will behave properly toward outsiders, without being dependent on anyone … but must work, doing good with his own hands, that he may have something to share with the one in need … If anyone does not provide for his own, and especially his own household, he has denied the faith and is worse than an unbeliever” (Exod 20:9; 1 Cor 7:20; 2 Thess 3:10–12; 1 Thess 4:11–12; Eph 4:28; 1 Tim 5:8). Indeed, our Lord Jesus Christ was a plain, honest carpenter, and worked hard in that particular calling until He entered on the public ministry, as all the ancients agree (Mark 6:3; Matt 13:55–56). And we read also that all the patriarchs had their particular callings. Abel was a keeper of sheep (Gen 4:2); Noah was a farmer (Gen 9:20); the sons of Jacob were shepherds and keepers of cattle (Gen 46:34); and all the apostles, before they were called to the work of the ministry, had their particular callings.

By the law of Muhammad, the Great Turk himself is bound to practice some manual trade or occupation.

Solon made a law that a son should not be bound to support his father in old age unless the father had set him to some occupation in his youth. And at Athens every man gave a yearly account to the magistrate of the trade or course of life by which he maintained himself; and if he could not, he was banished. And all writers condemn it as a very great vanity in Dionysius, who insisted on being the best poet, and in Caligula, who insisted on being the best orator, and in Nero, who insisted on being the best fiddler; and so they became the three worst princes, by minding other men’s business more than their own particular calling. But for a man to evade or neglect private prayer under pretense of his particular calling agrees with no scripture; indeed, it is contrary to very many scriptures, as is evident from the many arguments cited earlier.

Certainly no man’s calling is a calling away from God or godliness. It never entered the heart of God that our particular callings should ever drive our general calling of Christianity out of doors. Just as our general calling must not eat up our particular calling, so our particular calling must not eat up our general calling.

Certainly our particular calling must give way to our general calling. Did not the woman of Samaria leave her water jar, and run into the city, and say, “Come, see a man who told me everything I ever did. Could this be the Christ?” (John 4:28–29). Did not the shepherds leave their flocks in the field, and go to Bethlehem, and declare the good news of great joy that they had heard from the angel, that there was born that day in the city of David a Savior, who was Christ the Lord (Luke 2:8–21)? And did not Christ commend Mary for that holy neglect of her particular calling when she sat at His feet and heard His word (Luke 10:38ff)? And what do all these instances show, but that our particular callings must give the place of honor to the general calling of Christianity?

Certainly the works of our general calling are far greater and more glorious, more eminent and excellent, higher and nobler, than the works of our particular callings; and therefore it is much more tolerable for our general calling to borrow time from our particular calling than for our particular calling to borrow time from our general calling. Certainly those men are very ignorant or very profane who either think themselves so closely tied to follow their particular callings six days a week that they must not concern themselves with any religious services, or who think their particular callings to be a gulf or a grave designed by God to swallow up private prayer. God, who is the Lord of time, has reserved some part of our time to Himself every day. Though the Jews were commanded to labor six days of the week, yet they were also commanded to offer up a morning and evening sacrifice daily (Deut 6:6–8; Exod 29:38–39; Num 28:3).

The Jews divided the day into three parts:

The first for prayer;

The second for the reading of the law;

And the third for the works of their lawful callings.

As bad as the Jews were, yet every day they set part of the day apart for religious exercises. Certainly they are worse than Jews who spend all their time on their particular callings, and shut closet prayer quite out of doors. Certainly that man’s soul is in a very bad state who is so entangled with the encumbrances of the world that he can spare no time for private prayer. If God is the Lord of your blessings, the Lord of your time, and the Lord of your soul, how can you with any fairness or honor put off His service under a pretense of much business? That man is lost, that man is cursed, who can find time for anything but none to meet with God in his closet. That man is doubtless on the brink of ruin whose worldly business eats up all thoughts of God, of Christ, of heaven, of eternity, of his soul, and of his soul’s concerns.

==== 8. The More Business Lies on Your Hands, the More Need to Keep Close to Your Closet

Eighthly and lastly, I answer: the more worldly business lies on your hands, the more need you have to keep close to your closet. Much business lays a man open to many sins, and to many snares, and to many temptations. Now the more sins, snares, and temptations a man’s business lays him open to, the more need that man has to be much in private prayer, so that his soul may be kept pure from sin, and his foot may not be caught in the devil’s trap, and he may stand fast in the hour of temptation.

Private prayer is so far from being a hindrance to a man’s business that it is the way of ways to bring down a blessing from heaven on a man’s business (Ps 1:2–3; 127:1–2; 128:1–2), as the firstfruits that God’s people gave to Him brought down a blessing from heaven on all the rest (Deut 26:10–11). Whetting is no hindrance. Prayer and provender never hinder a journey.

Private prayer is like Jacob, who brought down a blessing from heaven on all that Laban had (Gen 30:27, 30). Private prayer gives a man a sanctified use both of all his earthly comforts and of all his earthly business. David and Daniel found this by experience, and therefore their great public duties could not draw them away from their private duties. Time spent in heavenly employments is no time lost from worldly business (Deut 28:1–8). Private prayer makes all we take in hand successful. Closet prayer has made many rich, but it never made any man poor or a beggar in this world.

No man on earth knows what the emergencies or the events of a day may be. “Do not boast about tomorrow, for you do not know what a day may bring forth” (Prov 27:1). Every day is, as it were, a great-bellied day; every day is, as it were, with child of something. But what it will bring forth, whether a cross or a comfort, no man can tell. Just as no one can tell, while a woman is with child, what kind of birth it will be, so no man knows what mercies a day may bring forth. No man knows what miseries a day may bring forth. No man knows what good a day may bring forth. No man knows what evil a day may bring forth. No man knows what afflictions a day may bring forth. No man knows what temptations a day may bring forth. No man knows what liberty a day may bring forth. No man knows what bonds a day may bring forth. No man knows what good success a day may bring forth. No man knows what bad success a day may bring forth. And therefore a man needs to be in his closet with God every day, so that he may be prepared and fitted to receive and make use of all the events, successes, and emergencies that may meet him in the course of his life. And let this much suffice as an answer to this first objection.

=== Objection 2: We Are Servants, and Have No Time We Can Call Our Own

Secondly, others may object and say, “Sir, we grant that private prayer is an indispensable duty that lies upon the people of God. But we are servants, and we have no time that we can call our own. Our master’s business is such that it will not allow us any time for private prayer, and therefore we hope we may be excused.”

==== 1. The Text Is General, and Not Limited to Any Sort or Rank of Persons

First, the text is general and not limited to any sort or rank of persons, whether high or low, rich or poor, slave or free, servant or master. “But you, when you pray, go into your closet, and when you have shut your door, pray to your Father, who is in secret” (Matt 6:6). Here are three singular #emph[yous], you, you, you, which are to be understood generally. You servant as well as you master, you slave as well as you free man, you poor man as well as you rich man, you maid as well as you mistress, you child as well as you father, you wife as well as you husband. Private prayer is an indispensable duty that lies upon all sorts and ranks of persons. A man may as well say that the singular pronoun #emph[you] that runs through the Ten Commandments—“You shall have no other gods before Me. You shall not make for yourself an idol…You shall not bow down to them or worship them…You shall not take the name of the #smallcaps[Lord] your God in vain…Six days you shall labor…You shall not murder. You shall not commit adultery. You shall not steal. You shall not bear false witness against your neighbor. You shall not covet your neighbor’s house. You shall not covet your neighbor’s wife, or his manservant or maidservant, or his ox or donkey, or anything that belongs to your neighbor” (Exod 20:3–17)—relates to the rich and not to the poor, to masters and not to servants, to the free and not to those who are in bonds, as he may say that the three #emph[yous] in the text relate to the rich and not to the poor, to masters and not to servants, to those who are free but not to those who are bound. But certainly there is no man in his right mind who will say so, who will affirm such a thing. Doubtless this pronoun #emph[you] reaches every man, whatever his rank or standing in this world.

==== 2. The Former Answers Are Here Very Applicable

Secondly, I answer that the first, the third, the fourth, the fifth, the sixth, the seventh, and the eighth answers given to the first objection are here very applicable. And oh, that all masters and servants were so wise, so serious, and so fair-minded as to lay all those answers warm on their own hearts! It might be a means to prevent much sin, and to move masters and mistresses to give their pious servants a little more time to lift up their hearts to Christ in a corner.

==== 3. If You Have Liberty to Choose, Change Your Station

Thirdly, I answer: if you are a servant who has liberty to choose a new master, you had better change your station than live under the roof of a master who is such an enemy to God, to Christ, to religion, to himself, and to the eternal welfare of your poor soul that he will not give you half an hour a day to spend in your room, your closet, though the glory of God, the good of his own family, and the everlasting happiness of your own soul are at stake in it (Ps 84:10; 120:5). It is better for you to change your master than to neglect your duty. “Were you a servant when you were called? Do not let it concern you—but if you can gain your freedom, take the opportunity” (1 Cor 7:21). We lost our liberty by sin, and by nature we desire nothing more than liberty.

The rabbis say of liberty, “If the heavens were parchment, the sea ink, and every blade of grass a pen, its praises could not be contained or expressed.” Laban’s house was full of idols. Great houses often are. Jacob’s tent was little, but the true worship of God was in it. It is infinitely better to live in Jacob’s tent than in Laban’s house. It is best to be with masters where we may have the least of sin and the most of God, where we may have the most helps, the best examples, and the choicest encouragements to be holy and happy.

The religious servant should be as careful in the choice of his master as the religious master is in the choice of his servant. Gracious servants are great blessings to the families where they live; and that master may well be called the unhappy master who will rather part with a gracious servant than spare him a little time in a day to pour out his soul before the Lord in a corner.

==== 4. A Gracious Servant Is Given a Principle by God to Cry, Abba, Father

Fourthly, I answer: if you are a gracious servant, then God has moved you by His Spirit and given you a principle of grace for this very purpose, that you may cry, Abba, Father, when you are alone, when you are in a corner, and no eye sees you but His Who sees in secret (Rom 8:15; Gal 4:6; 1 Cor 6:19; 2 Tim 1:14). If you are a gracious servant, then you have received not the spirit of the world but the Spirit who is from God (1 Cor 2:12). Now, he who has this tree of life also has the fruit that grows upon this tree. “But the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, and self-control,” etc. (Gal 5:22–23). Now, grace is called not the #emph[works] of the Spirit but the #emph[fruits] of the Spirit,

(1) Because all grace is derived from the Spirit as the fruit is derived from the root. And,

(2) To note the pleasantness and delightfulness of grace, for what is more pleasant and delightful than sweet and wholesome fruits (Song 4:16; 6:2)?

(3) To note the profit and advantage that flows to those who have the Spirit. For as many grow rich by the fruits of their gardens and orchards, so many grow rich in grace, in holiness, in comfort, in spiritual experiences, by the fruits of the Spirit. Now why has God given you His Spirit, and why has He laid into your soul a stock of supernatural graces, but that you may be in every way qualified, disposed, and fitted for private prayer, and to maintain secret communion with God in a corner?

Certainly, God never gave any poor servant a talent of gifts or a talent of grace except so that he might drive a secret trade heavenward.

==== 5. Daniel Was Both a Subject and a Servant to King Darius

Fifthly, I answer: though King Darius had made a decree that none should ask any petition of any god or man for thirty days, on the penalty of being cast into the den of lions, yet Daniel kept up his private devotions; and Daniel was both a subject and a servant to King Darius, and one on whose hands the chief and greatest affairs of the kingdom lay. In the first and second verses of the sixth chapter of Daniel, you will find that Daniel had an abundance of great and weighty employments on his hands. He was set over the whole affairs of the whole empire of Persia. He and two other presidents, of whom he himself was chief, were to receive the accounts of the whole kingdom from all those hundred and twenty princes who were employed in all public business in the Persian monarchy. And yet, notwithstanding such a multitude of business as lay on his hands, and notwithstanding his condition as a servant, he was very careful to redeem time for private prayer. Indeed, it is very noteworthy that the heart of Daniel, in the midst of all his mighty business, was so much set upon private prayer, upon his secret retirements for religious exercises, that he ran the risk of losing all his honors, profits, pleasures, yes, and life itself, rather than be deprived of convenient time and opportunities to wait upon God in his room. Certainly, Daniel will one day rise in judgment against all those subjects and servants who think to evade private prayer by their pleas of much business and of their being servants.

==== 6. What Singular Thing Do You Do? What Do You Do More Than Others?

Sixthly, I answer: if you#super[+] who are gracious servants cannot, notwithstanding your masters’ business, redeem a little time to wrestle with God in a corner, what singular thing do you do? What do you#super[+] do more than others? Do you#super[+] hear sermons? So do others. Do you#super[+] read? So do others. Do you#super[+] follow your masters to public prayers? So do others. Do you#super[+] join with your masters in family prayers? So do others. Oh, but gracious servants should go beyond all other servants in the world; they should do singular things for God. “What are you#super[+] doing more than others?” (Matt 5:47)—τί περισσὸν ποιεῖτε; What extraordinary thing are you#super[+] doing? What is more ordinary than to find servants following their masters to public prayers and to family prayers? Oh, but to find poor servants redeeming a little time from their masters’ business to pour out their souls before the Lord in a corner—this is not ordinary; indeed, this is extraordinary, and this wonderfully well becomes gracious servants. Oh, that all men’s servants who are servants to the Most High God would seriously consider,

(1) How singularly they are #emph[privileged by God] above all other servants in the world. They are called, adopted, reconciled, pardoned, justified before the throne of God, which other servants are not (1 Cor 3:22–23). And why then should not such servants be singular in their services, who are so singular in their privileges?

(2) Secondly, gracious servants are made #emph[partakers of a more excellent nature] than other servants are. “Through these He has given us His precious and magnificent promises, so that through them you#super[+] may become partakers of the divine nature” (2 Pet 1:4). The apostle in this expression does not aim at any essential change and conversion of our substance into the nature of God and Christ, but only at the raising and dignifying of our nature by Christ. That real, that near, that dear, that choice, that mysterious, that special, that singular union that Christians have with Christ raises them up to a higher resemblance and likeness of God and Christ than they ever had attained in their original perfection. Yet it does not bring in any real transformation of either our bodies or our souls into the divine nature. It is certain that our union and conjunction with Christ neither mingles persons nor unites substances, but it joins our affections and brings our wills into a league of friendship with Christ. To be made a partaker of the divine nature means two things, say some:

#emph[First], a fellowship with God in His holiness;

#emph[Secondly], a fellowship with God in His blessedness, in the beatific vision and brightness of glory.

To be made “partakers of the divine nature,” say others, is to be made partakers of those holy graces, those divine qualities, which are at times called the image of God, the likeness of God, the life of God, etc. (Eph 4:24; Col 3:10), by which we resemble God, not only as a picture resembles a man in outward features, but as a child resembles his father in face and disposition. Now, take the words whichever way you will, how highly it concerns those servants who are made partakers of the divine nature to do singular things for God, to do such things for God as other servants, who are not partakers of the divine nature, have no mind, no heart, no spirit to do—indeed, that they refuse and scorn to do!

(3) Thirdly, gracious servants are #emph[worthily descended]\; they have the most illustrious ancestry and honorable origin (1 John 5:19; John 3:8; Jas 2:5).

(4) Fourthly, gracious servants are #emph[worthily attended]; they are nobly guarded (Ps 34:15; Heb 1:14; Deut 33:26–27; Zech 2:5).

(5) Fifthly, gracious servants are #emph[worthily dignified]\; they are dignified with the highest and most honorable titles (1 Pet 2:9; Rev 1:5–6; 5:10).

(6) Sixthly, take many things in one: gracious servants have #emph[more excellent graces], experiences, comforts, communions, promises, assurances, revelations, hopes, helps, principles, food, clothing, portion, than all other servants in the world have. And therefore God may well expect better and greater things from them than from all other servants in the world. God may very well expect that they should do singular things for His glory, Who has done such singular things for their good. Certainly God expects that gracious servants should be blessing Him when other servants are blaspheming Him; that they should be magnifying Him when other servants are debasing Him; that they should be redeeming precious time when other servants are trifling, fooling, playing, or sinning away precious time; that they should be weeping in a corner when other servants are sporting and making themselves merry among their jovial companions; that they should be mourning in secret when other servants are sinning in secret; and that they should be at their private devotions when other servants are sleeping and snoring.

Solomon, the wisest prince who ever sat upon a throne, and who was guided by an infallible Spirit, laid it down as a standing maxim more than two thousand years ago that “the righteous man is more excellent than his neighbor” (Prov 12:26). When Solomon dropped this aphorism from his royal pen, there was not a man in the world who was legally righteous, Adam and all his posterity having fallen from all their honor, glory, dignity, and excellence into a most woeful gulf of sin and misery. And therefore Solomon must be understood of him who is evangelically righteous (Ps 14:1–3; Rom 3:9–12; Lam 5:16). He who is evangelically righteous, be he master or servant, rich or poor, slave or free, high or low, is more excellent than his neighbor. And oh, that all masters would seriously consider this, so that they may no longer behave so proudly, so loftily, so scornfully, so perversely, so distantly, so sourly, so bitterly, so harshly towards their pious servants as not to afford them a little time to pour out their souls before the Lord in a corner!

I have read of Ingo, an ancient king of the Draves and Veneds, who, making a stately feast, appointed all his pagan nobles to sit in the hall below, and at the same time commanded certain poor Christians to be brought up into his presence chamber to sit with him at his table, so that they might eat of his royal fare. When many wondered at this, he told them that he counted Christians, however poor, a greater ornament at his table and more worthy of his company than the greatest nobles who were not converted to the Christian faith. “For,” he said, “when these pagan nobles are thrust down to hell, these poor Christians shall be my companions and fellow princes in heaven.” Certainly, this noble prince will one day rise in judgment against all sour, churlish Labans who behave so harshly and so severely towards their gracious servants that they will not allow them a little time to wait upon God in a nook (Eph 6:9).

Why should not gracious masters give their gracious servants a little time for closet prayer now, considering that they are sharers with them in all the fundamental good that comes by Christ in this world, and considering that they shall be partakers with them in all the glory of another world? The poorest servant in a family has a soul more precious than heaven and earth; and the greatest work that lies on his hands in this world is to look to its eternal safety and security. For if that is safe, all is safe. If that is well, all is well. But if that is lost, all is lost. Every gracious servant, however poor and lowly he may be, has the image of God, the image of the King of kings, stamped upon him. And woe to him who wrongs, or despises, or tramples upon that image! Certainly, God Himself is wronged by the injury that is done to His image. The contempt and spite that is shown to the image or coin of a king is shown to the king himself, and accordingly he will avenge it.

If it was a capital crime in Tiberius’ days to carry the image of Augustus on a ring or coin into any filthy place, as Suetonius says it was, what a crime it must be in those masters who despise, revile, reproach, scorn, abuse, and tread under foot such servants as have the image of the great God stamped upon their souls, and all because they look Godward, Christward, heavenward, holinessward, dutyward! Masters should never taunt their servants with their inferiority, penury, poverty, misery, lowly parentage, or servile condition, but should remember that these things are more the Creator’s pleasure than the servant’s fault, and that the God who has made the master rich and the servant poor can as quickly make the master poor and the servant rich (Prov 22:2; 17:5). God many times puts down the mighty from their seats and exalts those of low degree (Luke 1:52). Certainly, no master or mistress should dare to insult or triumph over such servants as have souls as noble as their own. But they should seriously and frequently consider Solomon’s aphorism, “The righteous man,” though a servant, though the lowliest among all the servants, “is more excellent than his neighbor” (Prov 12:26), and accordingly give them a little time and liberty to converse with God in secret. And oh, that all gracious servants would show themselves to be more excellent than their neighbors: by making private prayer more a matter of conscience than their neighbors do, by being more in their closets than their neighbors are, by delighting themselves in their secret retirements more than their neighbors will, and by redeeming some time for God, for their souls, and for eternity, more than their neighbors do!

==== 7. God Is the Only Lord of Time

Seventhly, I answer that God is the only Lord of time. Time is more the Lord’s than it is your master’s, and therefore it is no neglect of your master’s business to take a little time daily for private prayer. Times belong to providence as well as outcomes; and as God is the God of our blessings, so He is the Lord of our times. “My times are in Your hands,” says David (Ps 31:15). Not only the times of his sorrows but also the times of his comforts, not only the times of his miseries but also the times of his blessings, not only the times of his dangers but also the times of his duties, were in the hands of God.

It is noteworthy that the psalmist does not say time, but times, in the plural, to show that every point and period of time depends upon the hand of God.

One, complaining of those who say, “Come, let us talk together, to pass away the time,” cries out with grief of spirit, #emph[O donec praetereat hora], “Oh, until the hour is gone! Oh, until the time is past which the mercy of your Maker has bestowed on you to perform repentance, to procure pardon, to gain grace, and to obtain glory.” The servant who borrows a little time every day to seek the face of God in a corner borrows it from God rather than from his master; and therefore why should his master fume, rage, or complain, considering that God never made him lord of time?

==== 8. Redeem Time from Sleep, Recreations, and Meals Rather Than Neglect the Closet

Eighthly, I answer that servants should rather redeem time from their sleep, their recreations, their daily meals, than neglect closet duty for a day. And certainly those servants who, out of conscience towards God and out of a due regard for the internal and eternal welfare of their own souls, every day redeem an hour from their sleep, or sports, or meals, to spend with God in secret, will find by experience that the Lord will make a few hours’ sleep sweeter and better to them than many hours’ sleep. Their outward sports shall be made up with inward delights, and instead of their common bread God will feed them with the bread that came down from heaven.

Sirs, was not Christ His Father’s Servant? “Here is My Servant, whom I uphold, My elect (or choice one), in whom My soul delights (or is well pleased). I will put My Spirit on Him, and He will bring justice to the nations” (Isa 42:1). And did He not redeem time from His natural rest rather than omit private prayer? “Early in the morning, a great while before day, Jesus got up and slipped out to a solitary place to pray” (Mark 1:35). Christ spent the day in preaching, in healing the sick, in working miracles; and rather than let these noble works shut out private prayer, He rose a great while before day, so that He might have some time to wrestle with His Father in secret. “In those days Jesus went out to the mountain to pray, and He spent the night in prayer to God” (Luke 6:12). O sirs! Did Christ spend whole nights in private prayer for the salvation of your#super[+] souls, and will you think it too much to redeem an hour from your natural rest to seek and to serve Him in a corner, and to make sure the things of your everlasting peace?

The redeeming of time for private prayer is the redeeming of a precious treasure which, once lost, can never be fully recovered again. If riches should make themselves wings and fly away, they may return again, as they did to Job. If credit, and honor, and worldly greatness and renown should fly away, they may return again, as they did to Nebuchadnezzar. If success, and famous victories and conquests, should make themselves wings and fly away, they may return again, as they did to many of the Roman conquerors and others. But if time, which the poets paint with wings to show its fleetness and swiftness, flies from us, it will never return to us again.

A great lady \[Queen Elizabeth\] of this land cried out on her deathbed, “Call time again, call time again! A world of wealth for an inch of time!” But time past never was, nor ever could be, recalled.

The Egyptians drew the picture of time with three heads. The first was of a greedy wolf gaping for time past, because it has ravenously devoured even the memory of so many things past recalling. The second was of a crowned lion roaring for time present, because it holds the rule over all action, for which it calls aloud. And the third was of a deceitful dog fawning for time to come, because it feeds foolish men with many flattering hopes, to their eternal undoing. Oh, that all this might prevail with servants to redeem time for private prayer! And if my counsel might be taken, I would rather advise servants to redeem some time for private prayer from their sleep, or lawful recreations, or set meals, etc., than to spend in private prayer the time which their masters call their own, especially if their masters are unconverted and “poisoned by bitterness and captive to iniquity” (Acts 8:23); and that for these five reasons:

(1) First, because this may be #emph[a means to prevent much sin on the master’s side]. Masters who are in their unregenerate state are very apt to storm, and rage, and let fly against God, and Christ, and religion, and the profession of faith, etc., when they see their servants spend in private prayer, or in any other religious exercise, the time which in their understanding is their own and ought to be wholly spent in following their business. Now gracious servants should have such honorable respect, such tender affection, and such Christian compassion for their masters’ souls as to do to the utmost all that lies in them to keep their masters from bringing guilt upon their souls, or from making work for repentance, for hell, or for the Physician of souls (Jude 22–23).

The Persians, the Turks, and many Indians are so compassionate that they build hospitals not only for lame and diseased men, but also for birds, beasts, and dogs that are old, starved, or hurt. Oh, then, what tender compassion should gracious servants show towards their masters’ souls, which are jewels worth more than heaven and earth!

(2) Secondly, because this may be #emph[a means to convince the judgments and consciences of their masters] that there is some worth, some excellence, some sweetness to be found in private prayer and in other closet duties. For when masters observe their servants redeeming time for closet duties from their very sleep, recreations, dinners, and suppers, they will be ready to conclude that certainly there is more worth, more goodness, more sweetness, more excellence, more glory, more gain in closet duties than they have ever understood, felt, or experienced, and that their own poor servants are better and more righteous than themselves. Sozomen reports that the devout life of a poor captive Christian woman made a king and all his family embrace the faith of Jesus Christ. Good works convince more than miracles themselves.

I have read of one Pachomius, a soldier under the emperor Constantine, how, when his army was almost starved for lack of necessary provisions, he came to a city of Christians, and they of their own charity relieved them speedily and freely. Wondering at their free and noble charity, he inquired what kind of people these were whom he saw so bountiful. He was told that they were Christians, whose profession it is to hurt no man and to do good to every man. At this Pachomius, convinced of the excellence of this religion, threw away his arms and became a Christian, a saint. Just as husbands are sometimes won by the conduct of their wives without the word (1 Pet 3:1–2), so masters may sometimes be won by the gracious bearing and conduct of their servants without the word. The servant’s redeeming of time for private duties on the hardest and severest terms may be so blessed to the master that it may result in his conviction, conversion, and salvation. There is a maybe for it, and even a maybe should be sufficient encouragement for every gracious servant to do all he can to save the soul of his master from going down into the infernal pit.

(3) Thirdly, because the servant’s redeeming of time from his sleep, recreations, and meals for private prayer will most clearly and abundantly #emph[show the singular love, the great delight, and the high esteem that he has for private prayer]. We say that those children love their books well and delight much in learning who will be at their books when others have gone to their beds, and who will be at their books before others can get out of their beds. Certainly, they love private prayer well, and they delight much in closet communion with God, who will be praying when others are sleeping, and who will be addressing their souls to God in a corner before their mistress is dressing herself at the mirror, or their fellow servants are dressing themselves in the shop.

(4) Fourthly, because the servant’s redeeming of time for private prayer from his sleep, set meals, recreations, etc., may be of the greatest #emph[use to other fellow servants]: both to awaken them and to convince them that the things of religion are of the greatest and highest importance, and that there is no trade, or pleasure, or profit to compare with that private trade which is driven between God and a man’s own soul; and also to keep them from trifling or fooling away the time which is truly and properly their masters’ time, and which by the royal law of heaven ought to be spent solely and wholly in their service and business. For what fair-minded servant is there in the world who will not argue like this? “I see that such and such of my fellow servants will redeem time for private prayer and for other closet services from their very sleep, meals, recreations, etc., rather than borrow or make free with the time which my master says is his, etc. And why then should I be so foolish, so brutish, so mad, as to trifle, or idle, or play, or toy away the time that should be spent in my master’s service and for my master’s advantage?”

(5) Fifthly and lastly, because the servant’s redeeming of time for private prayer from his sleep, his meals, his recreations, etc., cannot but be #emph[infinitely pleasing to God], and that which will give him the most comfort when he comes to die. The more any poor heart acts contrary to flesh and blood, the more he pleases God. The more any poor heart denies himself, the more he pleases God. The more any poor heart acts against the stream of sinful examples, the more he pleases God. The more difficulties and discouragements a poor heart meets with in the discharge of his duty, the more love he shows to God. And the more love a poor heart shows to God, the more he pleases God. “Go and proclaim in the hearing of Jerusalem that this is what the #smallcaps[Lord] says: ‘I remember the devotion of your youth, your love as a bride, how you followed Me in the wilderness, in a land not sown. Israel was holy to the #smallcaps[Lord], the firstfruits of His harvest. All who devoured her found themselves guilty; disaster came upon them,’ declares the #smallcaps[Lord]” (Jer 2:2–3). God was very highly pleased and greatly delighted with the singular love and choice affections of His people towards Him when they followed after Him and kept close to Him in that wearisome and strange passage through the waste, howling wilderness. How well all these things fit the poor pious servant who redeems time for private prayer on the hardest terms imaginable, I shall leave the fair-minded reader to judge. And certainly, on a deathbed, no tongue can express, nor any heart conceive but his who feels it, the unspeakable comfort that closet duties will give to him who has been exercised in them on the hard terms now under consideration.

==== 9. Your Near and Dear Relation to God Calls Aloud for Private Prayer

Ninthly, I answer: if you are a gracious servant, then the near and dear relation between God and you, and the choice privileges that you share in, call aloud for private prayer (John 8:32–33, 36). As you are your master’s servant, so you are the Lord’s freeman. “For he who was a servant when he was called by the Lord is the Lord’s freeman. Conversely, he who was free when he was called is Christ’s servant. You#super[+] were bought at a price; do not become servants of men” (1 Cor 7:22–23)—that is, either when they command you#super[+] things forbidden by Christ, or forbid you things commanded by Christ, or when they would exercise a dominion over your faith or a lordship over your consciences. Do not allow yourselves in spiritual things to be brought into such bondage by any men or masters in the world as not to use the freedom and liberty that Christ has purchased for you#super[+] with His dearest blood (Gal 5:1; Col 2:20; Gal 2:4). No servants are to serve their masters in opposition to Christ; nor are any servants to serve their masters as spiritual masters; nor are any servants to serve their masters as supreme masters, but as subordinate masters (Eph 6:5–7). And as every gracious servant is the Lord’s freeman, so every gracious servant is the Lord’s friend (Isa 41:8; Jas 2:23; John 15:13–15). And as every gracious servant is the Lord’s friend, so every gracious servant is the Lord’s son (Gal 4:5–6; Rom 8:16). And as every gracious servant is the Lord’s son, so every gracious servant is the Lord’s spouse (Hos 2:19–20; 2 Cor 11:2).

And now I appeal to the consciences of all who have tasted that the Lord is gracious, whether the near and dear relation between the Lord and pious servants does not call aloud upon them to take every opportunity and advantage they possibly can to pour out their souls before the Lord in secret, and to acquaint Him in a corner with all their secret needs, and weaknesses, and wishes. And as gracious servants are thus nearly and dearly related to God, so gracious servants are very highly privileged by God. Gracious servants are as much freed from the reign of sin, the dominion of sin, and the condemning power of sin as gracious masters are (Rom 6:14). Gracious servants are as much freed from hell, from the curse of the law, and from the wrath of God as their gracious masters are (Rom 8:1). Gracious servants are as much adopted, as much reconciled, as much pardoned, as much justified, and as much redeemed as their gracious masters are (Gal 3:13). Gracious servants are as much heirs, heirs of God and joint heirs with Christ, as their gracious masters are. Gracious servants are as much a chosen generation, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, a people for God’s own possession, called out of darkness into His marvelous light, as their gracious masters are. And therefore, since they all alike share in all these great and glorious privileges which belong to saints as saints, they are beyond all doubt alike obliged and bound to all those duties which lie upon saints as saints, among which private prayer is one; and therefore they are to buckle down to this duty against all carnal reasons and objections whatever.

==== 10. The Promised Reward Lies as Open to the Servant as to the Master

Tenthly and lastly, I answer that the promised reward in the text lies as fair and as open to the servant as to the master, to the slave as to the free, to the peasant as to the prince. Whoever prays to his heavenly Father in secret, be he high or low, rich or poor, honorable or lowly, servant or master, shall receive an open reward. The reward in the text is not to be confined or limited to this or that sort or rank of men, but is to be extended to all ranks and sorts of men who make conscience of private prayer, of closet duties. “Servants, obey your#super[+] earthly masters with respect and fear and sincerity of heart, just as you would obey Christ. And do this not only to please them while they are watching, but as servants of Christ, doing the will of God from your#super[+] heart. Serve with good will, as to the Lord and not to men, because you#super[+] know that the Lord will reward each one for whatever good he does, whether he is slave or free…Servants, obey your#super[+] earthly masters in everything, not only to please them while they are watching, but with sincerity of heart and fear of the Lord. Whatever you#super[+] do, work at it with your whole being, for the Lord and not for men, because you#super[+] know that you will receive the reward of the inheritance from the Lord. It is the Lord Christ you#super[+] are serving” (Eph 6:5–8; Col 3:22–24).

Such servants as serve their masters faithfully, heartily, and in sincerity of spirit shall receive the reward of grace and the reward of the inheritance. The lowliest servant who is faithful in the service of his master shall receive the eternal inheritance as his recompense (Rom 8:15–17). The recompense of reward in the scripture last cited is not of merit but of sheer grace, because the inheritance belongs only to children, on account of their birth or adoption. Faithful servants shall from servants be made sons, and so enjoy the heavenly inheritance. Christ is so noble a master that He will not allow any service that has been performed to men out of conscience towards His command to pass unrewarded. Oh, how much more will He recompense pious servants for the spiritual services that they perform for His sake, for His glory! God is so liberal a paymaster that no man shall so much as shut the door, or kindle a fire upon His altar, or give a cup of cold water—one of the least, readiest, and humblest refreshments there are—but he shall be rewarded (Mal 1:10; Matt 10:42).

It is an excellent observation of Calvin on God’s rewarding of the Rechabites’ obedience (Jer 35:19): “God,” he says, “often recompenses the shadows and seeming appearances of virtue, to show the pleasure He takes in the ample rewards that He has reserved for true and sincere piety.” Nebuchadnezzar, though a tyrant, yet being employed in God’s service against Tyre, shall have Egypt as his pay for his pains at Tyre (Ezek 29:18–20). It is an ancient slur and slander cast upon God, as if He were a harsh master, an ungenerous lord, and as if there were nothing to be got in His service but knocks, blows, wounds, afflictions, losses, etc., whereas He is a rewarder not only “of those who earnestly seek Him” but even of the very worst of men who do any service for Him (Heb 11:6). I have read of Herod Agrippa, the same who was struck by the angel and eaten by worms because he did not give glory to God (Acts 12:23), that when he was bound in chains and sent to prison by Tiberius for wishing Caius were emperor, one Thaumastus, a servant of Caius, carrying a pitcher of water, met him. Agrippa, being very thirsty, asked him to give him some of his water to drink, which he willingly did; at which Agrippa said, “This service you have done in giving me drink shall do you good another day.” And he was as big and as good as his word. For afterwards, when Caius was emperor and Agrippa was made king of Judea, Agrippa first got Thaumastus his liberty, then made him chief officer of his household, and at his own death arranged that Thaumastus should continue in the same office under his son. Now how much more will the King of kings reward all those poor pious servants of His who not only give Him cups of cold water in His members, but also redeem time from their very rest, meals, and recreations, so that they may have some time to seek the face of God in a corner!

Certainly, there shall not be a sigh, a groan, a prayer, a tear let fall by a poor servant in a corner that shall not at last be regarded and rewarded by the great God. Lyra says that Mordecai waited six years before his good service was rewarded by King Ahasuerus. It may be that God will reward you sooner for all your closet services; but if He does not reward you sooner, He will certainly reward you better. He will reward you with higher honors, with greater dignities, with more glorious robes, and with a more royal crown, even an incorruptible crown, a crown of righteousness, a crown of life, a crown of glory (1 Cor 9:25; 2 Tim 4:8; Rev 2:10; Jas 1:12; 1 Pet 5:4). And therefore hold on and hold out in your#super[+] secret retirements. Though some may deride you#super[+], and others revile you, and your carnal masters discourage you, yet God is faithful and will certainly reward you. Indeed, He will openly reward you#super[+] for all the secret pourings out of your souls in His bosom.

=== Objection 3: We Lack Those Gifts and Endowments That Others Have

Some may further object and say, Oh, but we cannot pray alone; we lack those gifts and endowments that others have. We are shut up and do not know how to pour out our souls before God in a corner. We would willingly pray, but we lack the ability to pour out our souls before the Lord in secret.

==== 1. God’s Dearest Children May Sometimes Be Shut Up

First, God’s dearest children may sometimes be shut up; they may, like Zechariah, be struck dumb for a time and not able to speak (Luke 1:20). “I am too troubled to speak…O Lord, my every desire is before You; my groaning is not hidden from You” (Ps 77:4; 38:9). God’s dearest children have sometimes been so shut up that they have been able to say nothing, nor to do anything but groan. A child of God may sometimes meet with such a blow from God, from conscience, from Scripture, from Satan, from the world, as may for a time so stun him that he may not be able to speak to God, nor speak to others, nor speak to his own heart. Just as the Holy Spirit is not always a teaching Spirit, nor always a leading Spirit, nor always a comforting Spirit, nor always a sealing Spirit, nor always a witnessing Spirit, nor always an assuring Spirit to any of the saints, so He is not always a supplicating Spirit in any of the saints. When He is grieved, vexed, quenched, provoked, He may suspend His gracious influences and deny the soul His assistance. And what can a Christian then say or do?

==== 2. There May Be the Spirit of Adoption in Sighs and Groans

Secondly, I answer, you cannot pray; but can you not sigh, nor groan either? There may be the Spirit of adoption in sighs and groans, as well as in spoken prayer (Rom 8:26). The force, the power, the efficacy, the excellence of prayer does not consist in the number and flourish of words, but in the supernatural stirrings of the Spirit—in sighs, and groans, and pangs, and strong affections of heart that are unspeakable and unutterable. Certainly, the very soul of prayer lies in the pouring out of a man’s soul before the Lord, though it be only in sighs, groans, and tears (1 Sam 1:13–19). One sigh and groan from a broken heart is more pleasing to God than all human eloquence.

==== 3. Beg God to Teach You to Pray

Thirdly, I answer, beg God to teach you to pray. Oh, beg for the Holy Spirit, Who is a Spirit of prayer. God has promised His Holy Spirit to those who ask for it. “If you#super[+] then, who are evil, know how to give good gifts to your children, how much more will your Father in heaven give the Holy Spirit to those who ask Him!” (Luke 11:13). “I will give you#super[+] a new heart and put a new spirit within you; I will remove your heart of stone and give you a heart of flesh. And I will put My Spirit within you#super[+] and cause you to walk in My statutes and to carefully observe My ordinances…And I will give them singleness of heart and put a new spirit within them; I will remove their heart of stone and give them a heart of flesh…I will pour out on the house of David and on the people of Jerusalem a spirit of grace and supplication” (Ezek 36:26–27; 11:19; Zech 12:10).

Now gracious promises are God’s bonds, and He loves to see His people sue on them. God expects us to put Him in remembrance, and to pray over His promises (Isa 62:6–7; Isa 43:25–26). When He had promised great things to His people concerning justification, sanctification, and preservation, He adds, “For this, too, I will be inquired of by the house of Israel, to do it for them” (Ezek 36:37).

God expects us to spread His gracious promises before Him, as Hezekiah did Sennacherib’s letter (Isa 37:14). God is never better pleased than when His people importune Him in His own words and urge Him with arguments taken from His own promises. Though God is a very affectionate father, and a very generous father, yet He is not a prodigal father, for He will never throw away His blessings on those who will not boldly and humbly plead His promises with Him. God loves to keep His royal state, and will be sought, both for His bestowing of blessings and for His making good of precious promises. You say you cannot pray. Why, can you not go into a corner and spread the promises just cited before the Lord, and tell Him how much it concerns His honor and glory, as well as your own internal and eternal good, to make good those gracious promises that He has made: His giving of His Spirit to those who ask Him, and His putting His Spirit within them, and His pouring out a Spirit of grace and supplication on them?

We read of Tamar that, when Judah her father-in-law lay with her, she took his signet, bracelets, and staff as a pledge. Afterward, when she was in great distress and ready to be burned as a harlot, she brought out her staff, and signet, and bracelets, and said, “I am pregnant by the man to whom these items belong,” and so she saved her life (Gen 38:18, 25). The promises are like so many rich mines; they are like so many choice flowers of paradise; they are the food, life, and strength of the soul. They are like a staff to support the soul, and they are like a signet and bracelets to adorn the soul and to enrich the soul. And therefore poor sinners should bring them out, and lay them before the Lord, and urge God with them, for there is no way on earth like this to save a man’s soul and to prevent a burning in hell. Concerning precious promises, let me give you#super[+] these eight hints:

#emph[First], that they are truly set forth and settled by God (Mark 10:30).

#emph[Secondly], that they shall certainly be performed, since they are all made in and through Christ (2 Cor 1:20). They are made first to Christ, and then to all who have union and communion with Him.

Sertorius, says Plutarch, paid what he promised with fair words, but God does not do so. Men many times say and unsay; they often eat their words as soon as they have spoken them. But God will never eat the words that have gone out of His mouth. “My purpose will stand, and all My good pleasure I will accomplish…Truly I have spoken, and truly I will bring it to pass. I have planned it, and I will surely do it” (Isa 46:10–11).

#emph[Thirdly], that they all flow from free grace, from special love, from divine goodness (Hos 14:4).

#emph[Fourthly], that they are all as unchangeable as He is who made them (Jer 31:3).

#emph[Fifthly], that they are all grounded and founded on the truth, faithfulness, and all-sufficiency of God (Mal 3:6).

#emph[Sixthly], that they are pledges and securities of great things that God will do for His people in time (Heb 13:5).

#emph[Seventhly], that they are most sure and certain evidences of divine favor, and a declaration of the heart and goodwill of God to His poor people (Heb 6:12; Num 23:19).

#emph[Eighthly], that they are the price of Christ’s blood.

Now, how all these things should encourage poor souls to be always pressing God with His promises!

==== 4. If You Cannot Pray as You Would, Pray as Well as You Can

Fourthly, you say you cannot pray, etc. Oh, that you would leave off objecting, and set to praying! If you cannot pray as you would, nor as you should, pray as well as you can. Joseph’s brothers stood so long dallying, and delaying, and trifling away the time that, having a journey to make to buy grain, they might have bought and returned twice before they went and bought once. When Elijah called Elisha, he beats around the bush, and he must first go and bid his father and mother farewell before he could follow the prophet (1 Kgs 19:20). O friends! Beware of dallying, delaying, trifling, and beating around the bush when you#super[+] should be setting to the work of prayer. What if, with Hannah, you can only weep out a prayer, or with Moses stammer out a prayer, or with Hezekiah chatter out a prayer? Yet do as well as you can, and you will find acceptance with God. “For if the eagerness is there, the gift is acceptable according to what one has, not according to what he does not have” (2 Cor 8:12). The tax collector’s prayer had not much rhetoric or eloquence in it—“God, have mercy on me, a sinner!” (Luke 18:13)—and yet God accepted it. He prayed much, though he spoke little, and God did not turn a deaf ear to him. That God who once accepted a handful of flour for a sacrifice, and a handful of goats’ hair for an offering, and the poor widow’s two mites as if they had been two millions, will certainly accept what you are able to do, though you fall short, yes, far short, of what you ought to do (Lev 2:1–2; 6:15; Luke 21:3). “Lord,” says Luther, “You command me to pray. I cannot pray as I would, yet I will obey; for though my prayer is not acceptable, yet Your own commandment is acceptable to You.” If weak Christians would only put forth in prayer the little strength they have, God would quickly renew their spiritual strength. He would certainly carry them on from strength to strength. He would always, by secret assistance and secret influences, help them on in their heavenly trade (Isa 40:29–31; Ps 84:7). As a loving, indulgent father will take his little child in his arms and carry him on in his way homeward when his strength begins to fail him, and he can walk no further, and the way proves dirty, slippery, or uneven, so God does with His own: “It was I who taught Ephraim to walk (as a nurse does the infant), taking them in My arms” (Hos 11:3). When God’s poor children come to a foul road or a rough place, He takes them up in His own arms, and helps them over the quagmire of crosses and the difficulties of duties, and over all that constraint, and narrowness, and weakness of spirit that attends them in their closet duties.

It is worth noting that when the king of Israel was to shoot the arrow, he put his hand on the bow, and Elisha put his hand on the king’s hand (2 Kgs 13:16). So when we go into our closets, we are to put up our hand, and then the Spirit of God likewise will put His hand on our hand. He will put His strength to our strength, or rather to our weakness. “In the same way, the Spirit helps us in our infirmities” (Rom 8:26): lifts with us, or helps together. The Greek word συναντιλαμβάνεται properly signifies such help as when another man of strength and ability steps in to sustain the burden that lies on our shoulders, be it a log or a piece of timber, setting his shoulders under it to lift it up and bear part of it with us; or to help us as the nurse helps her little child, holding it up by the sleeve. When a poor Christian sets himself to closet prayer, to mourn, to believe, or to obey, etc., then the Spirit comes in with new help, and new influences, and new assistance, and so carries him on in all these noble services. The child that only stammers at first will in time speak plainly and fluently. Oh, how many Christians there are who now can pray with much freedom, liberty, and fluency, who at first could only sigh out a prayer, or stammer out a prayer, or weep out a prayer!

You say you cannot pray; but if you would only stir yourself up to obey that command (Matt 6:6) as well as you can, you do not know but that a power may go forth with the command that may enable you to act in keeping with the command. In Matthew 9:1–9 Christ told the paralyzed man to rise and walk: “Get up, pick up your mat, and go home.” The paralyzed man might have objected, “Alas! I am carried by four. I am not able to stir a limb, much less to rise, and least of all to pick up my mat and walk.” Oh, but he roused himself up as well as he could, and a power went forth with the command that enabled him to do what was commanded. So there was a poor man who had a withered hand, and Christ commanded him to stretch out his hand (Matt 12:10–14). He might have replied, “My hand is withered, and if I might have as many worlds as there are men in the world for stretching it out, I could not stretch it out. Yes, if my very life—if my very salvation—depended on stretching out my withered arm, I could not stretch it out.” Oh! But he throws aside all such pleas and complies with Christ’s command as well as he could, and a power went forth and healed his hand.

O sirs! If you#super[+] would only pray in your closets as well as you can, you do not know but that such power and force might flow from Christ into your hearts as might carry you on in your closet duties beyond expectation, even to wonder. Others have found it so, and why not you#super[+]? Why not you#super[+]? Well! Remember that God is no exacting or critical observer of the awkward expressions that fall from His poor children when they are at their closet duties. He is such a Father as is very well pleased with the broken expressions and divine stammerings of His people when they are in a corner. It is not a flood of words, nor studied notions, nor seraphic expressions, nor elegant phrases in prayer that catches the ear, or that delights the heart of God, or that opens the gates of glory, or that brings down the best of blessings on the soul, but uprightness, holiness, heavenliness, spiritual-mindedness, and brokenness of heart. These are the things that make a conquest of God and that turn most to the soul’s profit.

==== 5. The Spirit of God Is a Spirit of Prayer and Supplication

Fifthly, you say you cannot pray; but if you are a child of God, you have the Spirit of God, and the Spirit of God is a Spirit of prayer and supplication. That all the children of God have the Spirit of God is most evident in the blessed Scriptures. Take these for a taste: “I will pour out on the house of David and on the people of Jerusalem a spirit of grace and supplication” (Zech 12:10). “Take not Your Holy Spirit from me” (Ps 51:11). “You#super[+] received the Spirit of adoption, by whom we cry, ‘Abba! Father!’” (Rom 8:15). “We have not received the spirit of the world, but the Spirit who is from God, that we may understand what God has freely given us” (1 Cor 2:12). “…the very One who gives you#super[+] His Holy Spirit” (1 Thess 4:8). “And by this we know that He remains in us: by the Spirit He has given us…By this we know that we remain in Him, and He in us: He has given us of His Spirit” (1 John 3:24; 4:13).

That all the children of God have the Spirit of God may be made further evident by an induction of these seven particulars.

\[1\] First, they are all sanctified by the Spirit. “You#super[+] were sanctified…by the Spirit of our God” (1 Cor 6:11). I do not say that they are all equally sanctified by the Spirit, but I say they are all really sanctified by the Spirit. Though all the servants of Christ have their talents, yet not all have their ten talents, nor all their five talents, nor all their two talents. Some have only their one talent (Matt 25:15). Though Benjamin’s portion was five times as much as his brothers’ portions, yet every one of his brothers had his portion (Gen 43:32–34). So though some Christians have five times more measures of the Spirit, and more measures of light, of love, of holiness, of heavenly-mindedness, than others have, yet every Christian has some measures of the Spirit and some measures of grace and holiness. Though some are babes in Christ and others are children in Christ, though some are young men in Christ and others old men in Christ, yet every one of them is born of the Spirit of Christ (1 Pet 2:2; 1 John 2:12–14; John 3:8). Though none of the people of God in this life has the Spirit in perfection, yet every one of them has so much of the Spirit as will bring him to salvation. Every Christian has so much of the Spirit as will bring Christ and his soul together; and therefore, beyond all doubt, every Christian has so much of the Spirit as will at last bring heaven and his soul together.

\[2\] Secondly, they are all led by the Spirit. “All who are led by the Spirit of God are sons of God” (Rom 8:14). Every child of God has a twofold guide: the Word without and the Spirit within (Isa 30:20–21). How the Spirit leads by the rule of the Word, and how He leads to God, and leads to Christ, and leads to truth, and leads to righteousness, and leads to holiness, and leads to happiness, I shall not now undertake to show (Prov 6:22; Eph 5:9).

\[3\] Thirdly, they are all upheld and strengthened by the Spirit. “Uphold me with Your free Spirit” (Ps 51:12), or underprop me or sustain me, as the Hebrew has it, with Your free, voluntary Spirit, or, as the Greek renders it, with Your noble, princely Spirit. “To be strengthened with power through His Spirit in the inner man” (Eph 3:16). By “the inner man” some understand the regenerate part of man; others understand by “the inner man” the soul with all its noble faculties and stirrings. Take the words whichever way you will, it is certain that all the spiritual might and strength that a Christian has, he has from the Holy Spirit. Though the Spirit strengthens every Christian in the inner man, yet I do not say that the Spirit strengthens every Christian alike in the inward man. Some have stronger corruptions to subdue than others, and more violent temptations to withstand than others, and greater difficulties to wrestle with than others, and choicer blessings to make use of than others, and higher and harder duties of religion to manage than others; and accordingly they are more strengthened in the inner man than others.

\[4\] Fourthly, they are all partakers of the firstfruits of the Spirit. “We ourselves, who have the firstfruits of the Spirit” (Rom 8:23), which are only like a handful of grain compared with the whole crop. All the grace and all the holiness that we have from the regenerating Spirit at our first conversion is only a drop to that sea, a mite to those talents, which we shall receive in the life to come (2 Cor 1:22).

\[5\] Fifthly, they are all taught by the Spirit. “The Holy Spirit, whom the Father will send in My name, will teach you#super[+] all things” (John 14:26; Isa 59:21).

This promise belongs primarily to the apostles, and secondarily to all believers.

Though these words were spoken at first to the apostles only, yet they were not spoken about the apostles only (Isa 54:13). In these words there are three things promised to the apostles:

First, immediate illumination by the Spirit of God.

Secondly, a full knowledge of all those truths that belonged to their apostolic office and that were necessary for them at that juncture of time.

Thirdly, absolute infallibility in matters of doctrine.

There are also three things promised to all believers:

First, mediate illumination: truths taught by the Spirit of truth in the use of the means of grace.

Secondly, knowledge of all truth necessary to salvation.

Thirdly, infallibility too, so far as they adhere and keep close to the Spirit’s teaching in the Word.

Philo says that the primitive Christians were called tillers, because, as farmers till their fields and manure their land, so they taught their families and nurtured their children and servants with good instruction. Oh, what choice teachings of the Spirit these primitive Christians were under, who made it so much their business, their work, to teach those who were under their charge (1 Thess 4:9; 2 Cor 3:8)! “But the anointing you#super[+] received from Him remains in you, and you do not need anyone to teach you. But just as His true and genuine anointing teaches you about all things” (1 John 2:27). Not that we know all things absolutely, or that we do not need a ministry to teach and instruct us; but he speaks comparatively: you#super[+] will not be so helped by any instruction without the Spirit as with the Spirit. The Spirit will declare the truth as it is in Jesus more clearly, more freely, more particularly, more certainly, more universally, more effectually, than any other is able to do. The Spirit, this holy anointing, shall teach the saints all things; not all things knowable, for that is impossible for finite creatures to attain to. Who knows the movements of the heavens, the influences of the stars, the nature of living creatures, or how the bones grow in the womb of her who is with child? Who knows the reason why the river Nile should overflow in the summer, when waters are at their lowest? Or why the lodestone should draw iron to it, or turn toward the pole star?

Pliny tells us of one who spent fifty-eight years in learning the nature of the bee, and yet had not fully attained it. How is it possible, then, for the wisest naturalist to enter into the deep things of God?

Paul, who learned his theology among the angels, and who had the Holy Spirit for his immediate teacher, tells us plainly that he knew only in part (1 Cor 13:9–11); and oh, then, how little a part of that part do we know! But the Spirit teaches the saints all things; that is,

First, He teaches them all things needful for the salvation of their souls, all things necessary to bring them to heaven (John 17:3).

Secondly, all things needful to life and godliness (2 Pet 1:3).

Thirdly, all things needful to their places, callings, sexes, ages, and conditions.

Fourthly, all things needful for you#super[+] to know to preserve you in the truth, and to preserve you from being deluded and seduced by those false teachers of whom he speaks (1 John 2:10, 19, 22–23, 26). And certainly this is the main thing that John hints at in that expression. The “all things” spoken of in verse 27, in Scripture’s usual way of speaking, must necessarily be understood only of all those things which are spoken of there.

\[6\] Sixthly, they are all comforted by the Spirit. “…living in the fear of the Lord and the comfort of the Holy Spirit…For the kingdom of God is not a matter of eating and drinking, but of righteousness, peace, and joy in the Holy Spirit…And you#super[+] became imitators of us and of the Lord when you welcomed the message with the joy of the Holy Spirit, in spite of your great suffering” (Acts 9:31; Rom 14:17; 1 Thess 1:6). Not that all Christians always have actual comfort, actual joy. Oh no! For as the air is sometimes clear and sometimes cloudy, and as the sea is sometimes ebbing and sometimes flowing, so the comforts and joys of the people of God are sometimes ebbing and sometimes flowing, sometimes clear and sometimes cloudy.

Hudson the martyr, feeling himself deserted by God at the stake, went out from under his chain, and having prayed earnestly, was comforted immediately and suffered valiantly.

So Mr. Glover the martyr felt himself deserted by God in prison; but as he was going to the stake he looked back and cried out to his friend, “He has come, He has come,” meaning the Comforter, and so he laid down his life with joy.

Rachel wept and would not be comforted. She gave so much way to weeping that she would not give the least way to comfort; and so it is many times with the choicest saints. “My soul refused to be comforted” (Ps 77:2). It is not my purpose at present to dwell on the various ways in which the people of God refuse comfort and fall short of those strong consolations which God is willing that they should receive. The sun may work where it does not shine, and a man may be in a state of salvation and yet lack consolation. A man may fear the Lord and obey the voice of His servant, and yet walk in darkness and see no light (Isa 50:10). There is no Christian who may not sometimes have trouble in his conscience, grief in his heart, tears in his eyes, and fears and questionings in his soul whether God is his Father, and whether Christ is his redeemer, and whether mercy belongs to him, yes, whether any promise in the book of God belongs to him.

Joy and comfort are those delicacies, those sweetmeats of heaven, that God does not feast His people with every day (Ps 30:6–7). Every day is not a wedding day, every day is not a harvest day, and every day is not a summer’s day. The fatted calf is not killed every day, nor the robe and the ring put on every day. Every day is not a festival day nor a dancing day (Luke 15:22–23; Eccl 3:4; Rom 12:15). As there is a time to sing, so there is a time to sigh. As there is a time to laugh, so there is a time to weep. And as there is a time to dance, so there is a time to mourn. All tears will never be wiped clean from our eyes until all sin is wholly taken out of our hearts.

But notwithstanding all this, gracious souls always have sure and choice grounds of consolation. They have the promises. They have the “firstfruits of the Spirit” (Rom 8:23). They have union with Christ. And they have a right to eternal life, though they do not always have felt comforts. The children of God always have cause to exercise faith and hope in God in their darkest condition, though they do not always have actual joy and consolation (Job 13:15; Ps 42:5). The Comforter always abides with the saints, though He does not always actually comfort the saints (John 14:16). The Spirit many times carries on His sanctifying work in the soul when He does not carry on His comforting work in the soul. The Spirit many times acts in a way of humbling when He does not act in a way of consolation. The Spirit many times fills the soul with godly sorrow when He does not fill the soul with holy joy. The actions of the Spirit, as to His comforting work, all depend on His own sovereign will and pleasure; and therefore He may abide in the soul when He does not actually comfort the soul.

\[7\] Seventhly, the people of God, first or last, are sealed by the Spirit. “In Him … after … you#super[+] believed, you were sealed with the Holy Spirit of promise” (Eph 1:13). The nature of sealing consists in the imparting of the image or character of the seal to the thing sealed. To seal a thing is to stamp the character of the seal on it. Now, the Spirit of God really and effectually imparts the image of God to us, and that image consists in righteousness and true holiness. We are truly sealed by the Spirit of God when the Holy Spirit stamps the image of grace and holiness so obviously, so evidently, on the soul that the soul sees it, feels it, and can run and read it. Then the soul is sealed by the Holy Spirit.

“And do not grieve the Holy Spirit of God, in whom you#super[+] were sealed for the day of redemption” (Eph 4:30). The person of the Holy Spirit is set forth here in the Greek with a very great force, such as our tongue is not able fully to express. Here are three words that have three articles, every word its own article by itself, τὸ πνεῦμα τὸ ἅγιον, τοῦ θεοῦ: “#emph[The] Spirit,” not “#emph[a] Spirit.” And not “holy,” but “#emph[the] holy.” Nor “of God,” but “of #emph[that] God.” “Who also placed His seal on us, and put His Spirit in our hearts as a pledge of what is to come” (2 Cor 1:22).

In these scriptures, you see that the Spirit is a seal. Now, a seal among men is,

First, for secrecy.

Secondly, for distinction.

Thirdly, for authority.

Fourthly, for certainty. A sealed writing is authentic, and serves to make things sure.

In the three texts last cited, if you compare them together, you may observe these six things:

First, the person sealing, and that is the #emph[Father].

Secondly, in whom, in #emph[Christ].

Thirdly, with what seal, the #emph[Spirit of promise]. There are all the persons of the Trinity making us sure of our inheritance.

Fourthly, when, #emph[after you#super[+] believed].

Fifthly, the end, which is twofold:

(1) #emph[Subordinate], and that is the certainty of our salvation;

(2) #emph[Ultimate], and that is the praise of His glory.


Sixthly, the time, how long this seal and pledge shall assure us, and that is, until we have complete possession of that of which it is a pledge.

To prevent mistakes and disputes about the sealings of the Spirit on the one hand, and to support, comfort, and encourage the poor people of God on the other hand, let me briefly touch on the Spirit’s #emph[special] sealing times.

\[1\] First, #emph[conversion times] are often the Spirit’s sealing times. On the prodigal’s return, the fattened calf is killed, the best robe is put on his back, the ring is put on his hand, and shoes on his feet (Luke 15:22–23). Some understand by the robe the royalty of Adam, others the righteousness of Christ. And by the ring some understand the pledges of God’s love, rings being given as pledges of love; others understand by the ring the seal of God’s Holy Spirit, since men used their rings as seals. Among the Romans the ring was a badge of virtue, honor, and nobility, by which those who wore them were distinguished from the common people. I think the main thing intended by the robe and the ring is to show us that God sometimes, on the sinner’s conversion and return to Him, is graciously pleased to give him some choice manifestations of His gracious pleasure and goodwill, and to seal up to him His everlasting love and favor. And this is why some who are only babes in Christ (1 Pet 2:2–3; 1 John 2:12–14) are so diligent and active in religious duties, and so conscientious and skillful in the exercise of their graces. At their first conversion, God helps some of His people to read their own names written in legible letters in the Book of Life (Acts 9:3–6). No sooner are some converted than the Spirit stamps His seal on them.

\[2\] Secondly, #emph[believing times] are sealing times (Eph 1:13). When they were in the very exercise of their faith, when they were acting their faith—for that is what the original implies—the Spirit came and sealed them up for the day of redemption (Rom 15:13; 1 Pet 1:8). He who honors Christ by frequent acts of faith in Him, him Christ will honor by setting His seal and mark on him.

\[3\] Thirdly, #emph[humbling times], mourning times, are sealing times. When a holy man was asked which were the most joyful days, the most comforting days, that he had ever enjoyed, he answered, his mourning days. His mourning days were his most joyful days, and therefore he cried out, “Oh, give me my mourning days, give me my mourning days, for they were my most joyful days!” Those were days in which God sealed up His everlasting love to his soul (Job 22:29; Isa 29:19). When the prodigal had greatly humbled himself before his father, then the best robe and the ring were put on him (Luke 15:17–24). None long for the sealings of the Spirit like humble souls; none set so high a price on the sealings of the Spirit as humble souls; and none make such choice use of the sealings of the Spirit as humble souls. And therefore, when men’s hearts are humble and low, the Spirit comes and sets the privy seal of heaven on them.

\[4\] Fourthly, #emph[sin-killing, sin-mortifying, sin-subduing times] are the Spirit’s sealing times. “To the one who overcomes, I will give the hidden manna. I will also give him a white stone inscribed with a new name, known only to the one who receives it” (Rev 2:17).

God will give the victorious Christian a secret love-token by which his soul may rest assured of the unspeakable love of God and of its freedom from condemnation. White stones were of very great use among the Romans and among the Athenians, and served to acquit the accused in courts of justice. When wrongdoers were accused, tried, and condemned in their courts, they gave them a black stone as a token of condemnation; but when they were acquitted, they gave them white stones as a token of acquittal. The Holy Spirit seems to allude to this practice. He who is victorious over his lusts shall have a new name that is better than the names of sons and daughters (Isa 56:5); and he shall have the pardon of his sins written in fair letters on the white stone, so that he may run and read his acquittal. The victorious Christian shall have assurance of the full discharge of all his sins. He shall have clear evidence of his justification and blessed assurance of his eternal election, all of which are hidden and mysterious things to all but those who have experienced and tasted what these sweetmeats of heaven mean (1 John 1:7).

Among the Romans, solemn feasts were held in honor of those who were victorious in their sacred games. Now those who were to be admitted to those feasts used to have their names written on white shells and white stones, and by these tickets they were admitted. Now some think the Holy Spirit alludes to this practice, and so would point us to a secret mark by which victorious Christians may be known and admitted as invited guests to the heavenly banquet of the hidden manna, according to Revelation 19:9. O sirs! When predominant lusts are brought under, when bosom sins lie slain in the soul, then the Spirit comes and seals up love, and life, and glory to the soul.

\[5\] Fifthly, #emph[suffering times] are sealing times (Acts 7:55–56, 59–60; Rev 1:9–10; 2 Cor 4:15–17). The early Christians found them so, and the suffering saints in Queen Mary’s days found them so.

When the furnace is seven times hotter than usual, the Spirit of the Lord comes and seals up a man’s pardon in his bosom, and his peace with God, and his title to heaven. When the world frowns most, then God smiles most. When the world puts its iron chains on the saints’ legs, then God puts His golden chains about the saints’ necks. When the world puts a bitter cup into one hand, then the Lord puts a cup of consolation into the other hand. When the world cries out, “Crucify them, crucify them!” then commonly they hear that sweet voice from heaven, “These are My beloved ones, in whom I am well pleased.”

Blessed Bradford looked on his sufferings as evidence to him that he was on the right way to heaven. And Ignatius says, “It is better for me to be a martyr than to be a monarch.”

\[6\] Sixthly, #emph[self-denying times] are the Spirit’s sealing times (Matt 19:27–29).

First, there is sinful self, which takes in a man’s lusts.

Secondly, there is natural self, which takes in a man’s skills, talents, gifts, and reason.

Thirdly, there is religious self, which takes in all a man’s religious duties and services, whether ordinary or extraordinary.

Fourthly, there is moral self, which includes freedom from gross, heinous, enormous wickedness, and a fair, sweet, harmless behavior toward men.

Fifthly, there is relative self, which takes in our nearest and dearest relations in the flesh, such as wife, children, father, mother, brothers, sisters, etc. (Ps 45:7–11).

Now when a man comes in this way to deny himself in all these things for Christ’s sake, and the gospel’s sake, and religion’s sake, then the Spirit of the Lord comes and seals him up for the day of redemption. This is a truth confirmed by the experiences of many martyrs now in heaven, and by the testimony of many Christians still alive.

\[7\] Seventhly, #emph[sacrament times] are sealing times. In that “feast … of choice meat” (Isa 25:6), God by His Spirit seals up His love to His people, and His covenant to His people, and pardon of sin to His people, and heaven and happiness to His people. There are many precious souls who have found Christ in this ordinance when they could not find Him in other ordinances, though they had sought Him with sorrow. In this ordinance, many a distressed soul has been strengthened, comforted, and sealed.

I might give you many instances. Take one for all. There was a gracious woman who, after God had filled her soul with comfort and sealed up His everlasting love to her, fell back into her former fears and trouble of spirit. Being at the Lord’s Supper, a little before the bread was given to her, Satan seemed to appear to her and told her that she must not presume to eat. But in that very nick of time, the Lord was pleased to bring into her mind that passage in the Song of Songs, “Eat, O friends” (Song 5:1). But notwithstanding this, Satan went on terrifying her, and when she had eaten, he told her that she must not drink. But then the Lord brought the second clause of the verse to her remembrance: “drink; drink freely” (or, “be drunk,” as the Hebrew has it), “O beloved” (or, “my loves,” as the Hebrew has it—all faithful souls are Christ’s loves). And so she drank also, and at once was filled with such unspeakable joys that she hardly knew how she got home. These soul-enrapturing joys continued for two weeks after and filled her mouth with songs of praise, so that she could neither sleep nor eat more than she forced herself to out of a conscientious sense of duty. At the two weeks’ end, when God was pleased to lessen her measure of joy, she came to a settled peace of conscience and assurance of the love of God, so that for twenty years after she had not so much as a cloud on her spirit, or the least questioning of her share in Christ.

\[8\] Eighthly, when God calls His people to some #emph[great and noble work], when He sets them on some high services, some difficult duties, some holy and eminent employments, then His Spirit comes and sets His seal on them. “Before I formed you in the womb I knew you, and before you were born I set you apart and appointed you as a prophet to the nations” (Jer 1:5). When the Lord sent the prophet Jeremiah to pronounce most dreadful judgments against a rebellious people, a shameless, brazen-faced nation, He assured him of his eternal election, and of His choice presence and singular help in the work that He set him to (vv. 8, 17-19). This is how the Lord dealt with Peter, James, and John (Matt 17:1–6), and this is how He dealt with Paul (Acts 9:1–23).

\[9\] Ninthly, when they are taken up into #emph[more than ordinary communion with God], then is the Spirit’s sealing time. When was it that the bride cried out, “My beloved is mine, and I am his!” but when Christ brought her to His banqueting house, and His banner over her was love? (Compare Song 2:16, 3–6, etc.)

\[10\] Tenthly and lastly, when Christians give themselves up to #emph[private prayer], when Christians are more than ordinarily exercised in secret prayer, in closet duties, then the Spirit comes and seals up the covenant and the love of the Father to them. When Daniel had been wrestling and weeping, and weeping and wrestling all day long with God in his closet, then the angel told him that he was a man “greatly beloved” of God, or a man of great desires, as the original has it (Dan 9:20–23). There was a gracious woman who, after much attending of sermons and walking in the ways of the Lord, fell into great spiritual desertions; but while she was in secret prayer, God came in with abundance of light and comfort, sealing up to her soul this part of His covenant: “I will remove their heart of stone and give them a heart of flesh, so that they may follow My statutes, keep My ordinances, and practice them. Then they will be My people, and I will be their God” (Ezek 11:19–20).

And so I have given you a brief account of the Spirit’s special sealing times. Now note this: God sets this seal on all His wares, on all His adopted children; for sooner or later there are none of His who are not sealed with this seal. God sets His seal of regeneration, He stamps His image of holiness on all His people, to mark them off and distinguish them from all profane, merely moral, and hypocritical persons in the world (John 3:3; 2 Thess 2:13; Heb 12:14). Doubtless the sanctifying work of the Holy Spirit, imprinting the outlines and features of God’s image of righteousness and holiness on man, as a seal or signet leaves an impression and stamp of its likeness on the thing sealed, is the seal of the Spirit spoken of in Scripture. “God’s firm foundation stands, bearing this seal: ‘The Lord knows those who are His,’ and, ‘Everyone who calls on the name of the Lord must turn away from iniquity’” (2 Tim 2:19).

But to prevent mistakes, you must remember that though the Spirit of the Lord, first or last, will set His seal on every real saint, yet the impression of that seal is not equally visible in all; for some bear this impression as babes, others as men grown up to some maturity. All God’s adopted children bear this impression truly, but none of them bear it perfectly in this life. Sometimes this seal of regeneration, this seal of holiness, is so plain and obvious that a man may run and read it in himself and others. And at other times it is so obscure and dark that he can hardly discern it, either in himself or in others. This seal is so vividly stamped on some of God’s people that it reveals itself very visibly, eminently, gloriously; but on others it is not equally visible. And so I have made it evident by these seven particulars that all the children of God have the Spirit of God.

Now note this: the Spirit of God that is in all the saints is a Spirit of prayer and supplication. “You#super[+] received the Spirit of adoption, by whom we cry, ‘Abba! Father!’” (Rom 8:15). While the child is in the womb, it cannot cry; but as soon as it is born, it cries. While Paul lay in the womb of his natural state, he could not pray; but no sooner was he born of the Spirit than the next news is, “Behold, he is praying” (Acts 9:11). Prayer is nothing but the turning of a man’s inside outward before the Lord. The very soul of prayer lies in the pouring out of a man’s soul into the bosom of God. Prayer is nothing but the breathing out before the Lord of what was first breathed into us by the Spirit of the Lord. Prayer is nothing but a choice, a free, a sweet, and intimate fellowship of the soul with God. Certainly, it is a great work of the Spirit to help the saints to pray.

“Because you#super[+] are sons, God sent the Spirit of His Son into your hearts, crying out, ‘Abba, Father!’” (Gal 4:6). God has no stillborn children. The doubling, “Abba, Father,” shows trusting, childlike, and vehement affection. The first is a Hebrew or Syriac word, the second a Greek one, and by this is signified the union of the Hebrews and the Greeks, or the Jews and Gentiles, in one church. “Abba, Father.” What is #emph[Abba]? Others say it is Father in Hebrew; and “Father” is added because in Christ the cornerstone both peoples are joined, alike becoming sons, wherever they come from: the circumcision from one place, hence #emph[Abba]\; the uncircumcision from another, hence #emph[Father] is named. The harmony of the walls is the glory of the cornerstone.

The word #emph[Abba], others say, means father in the Syriac tongue, which the apostle keeps here because it is a word full of affection, which young children keep in almost all languages when they begin to speak. And he adds the word #emph[Father], not only to explain it, but also the better to express the eager stirrings, the earnest and vehement desires, and the singular affections of believers in their crying to God, just as Christ Himself repeated the word #emph[Father] for the same purpose when He was in His greatest distress (Mark 14:36). This little word #emph[Father], says Luther, lisped out in prayer by a child of God, exceeds the eloquence of Demosthenes, Cicero, and all the other famous orators in the world.

It is certain that the Spirit of God helps the saints in all their communion with God, in their meditations on God, in their reading and hearing of the Word of God, in their communion with one another, and in all their solemn addresses to God. And for this the apostle gives us a most special instance in Romans 8:26: “In the same way, the Spirit helps us in our infirmities. For we do not know what we should pray for as we ought, but the Spirit Himself intercedes for us with groans too deep for words.”

When we are to pray, there is in us sometimes an infirmity of ignorance, so that we do not know what to pray for, either as to the matter or the manner. And at other times there is in us an infirmity of pride and conceit, so that we cannot pray with the humility and lowliness of spirit that we should, spiritual pride having fly-blown our prayers. Sometimes there is in us an infirmity of deadness, dullness, drowsiness, etc., so that we cannot pray with the warmth, heat, life, spirit, and fervency that we should or that we would. And at other times there is in us an infirmity of unbelief and slavish fears, so that we cannot pray with the faith and holy boldness that become children who draw near to a throne of grace and mercy. But now the Spirit helps these infirmities by way of instruction, prompting and teaching us what to pray for, and how we should spell our lesson. He tells us, as it were within, what we should say and how we should sigh and groan. He rouses and enlivens and stirs us up to prayer. By His singular influence and choice assistance He opens and enlarges our hearts in prayer. And by tuning the strings of our affections, He prepares us and fits us for the work of supplication. And therefore everyone who mocks the spirit of prayer in the saints, saying, “These are the men and the women who pray by the Spirit!” blasphemes against the Holy Spirit, since it is a main work of the Spirit to teach the saints to pray and to help them in prayer. Now, since all the saints have the Spirit, and the Spirit is a Spirit of prayer and supplication, there is no reason in the world why a saint should say, “I would pray in secret, but I cannot pray; I cannot pour out my soul or my complaint before the Lord in a corner.”

==== 6. You Can Manage Your Worldly Business as Well as Others

Sixthly and lastly, you say you cannot pray; you do not have the gifts and talents that others have. But you can manage your callings, your worldly business, as well as others; why then can you not pray as well as others? Ah, friends! If you#super[+] only loved private prayer as well as you love the world, and delighted in private prayer as much as you delight in the world, and if your hearts were as much set on closet prayer as they are set on the world, you would never say you could not pray; indeed, you would soon pray as well as others. It is not so much from lack of ability to pray in secret that you#super[+] do not pray in secret, as it is from lack of a will, a heart, to pray in secret, that you do not pray in secret. Jacob’s love for Rachel, and Shechem’s love for Dinah, carried them through the greatest difficulties (Gen 29; 34). If men’s affections were only strongly set on private prayer, they would quickly find abilities to pray. He who sets his affections on a young woman, though he is neither learned nor eloquent, will find words enough to let her know how his heart is taken with her. The application is easy. The man in Seneca complained of a thorn in his foot when his lungs were rotten. So many complain of a lack of ability to pray in their closets when their hearts are rotten. Sirs! Only get better hearts, and then you#super[+] will never say you can’t pray. It is one of the saddest sights in all the world to see men with strong talents and gifts for all worldly business crying out that they can’t pray, that they have no ability to pour out their souls before the Lord in secret. You#super[+] have sufficient talents and gifts to tell men of your sins, your needs, your dangers, your difficulties, your mercies, your deliverances, your duties, your trials, your losses, your enjoyments, your friends, your foes. Why then are you#super[+] not ashamed to complain of your lack of talents and gifts to tell those very things to God in a corner, which you can tell to men even on the housetops?

=== Objection 4: God Is Well Acquainted with All Our Needs

Fourthly, some may further object and say, “God is very well acquainted with all our needs, necessities, straits, and trials; and there is no moving Him to give us any favors that He does not intend to give us, whether we pray in our closets or not. So to what purpose do you press secret prayer so hard on us?”

To this objection I shall give these answers:

==== 1. This Objection Lies as Strong Against Family and Public Prayer

First, this objection lies as strong against family prayer and public prayer as it does against private prayer. God knows all your needs and necessities, all your straits and trials; so why need you pray in your family, why need you attend public prayers in the communion of saints? There is no wringing any mercy out of the hands of heaven which God does not intend to give. This objection confronts every kind of prayer and fights against every kind of prayer.

==== 2. Private Prayer Is Part of That Homage We Owe to God

Secondly, I answer that private prayer is a piece of divine worship and adoration; it is a part of the homage which we owe to God on account of a divine command, as I have already proved. Now, all objections must bow before the face of divine commands, as Joseph’s brothers bowed before him (Gen 42:6), or as King Ahasuerus’s servants bowed before Haman (Esth 3:2). Indeed, every objection that is raised against a divine command should fall before it, as Dagon fell before the ark, or as Goliath fell before David. He who casts off private prayer under any pretense whatever casts off the dominion of God, the authority of God, and this may be as much as a man’s life and soul are worth.

==== 3. Prayer Is the Silver Channel, the Golden Pipe of Mercy

Thirdly, I answer: though prayer is not the ground, the cause, of obtaining favors and mercies from God, yet it is the means. It is the silver channel, it is the golden pipe, through which the Lord is pleased to convey to His people all temporal, spiritual, and eternal favors (Ezek 36:26–37). God promises to give them the cream, the choicest, the sweetest of all spiritual, eternal, and temporal blessings. But note verse 37: “For this, too, I will be sought by the house of Israel, to do it for them.” Though God is very prompt and ready to give His people the best and the greatest of blessings, yet He will be sought by prayer for the actual enjoyment of them. He who has no heart to pray for a mercy he needs has no ground to believe that God will ever give him the mercy he needs. There is no receiving without asking, no finding without seeking, no opening without knocking. The threefold promise attached to the threefold command in Matthew 7:7 should encourage all Christians to be urgent, fervent, and constant in prayer. The proud beggar gets nothing from men, and the silent sinner gets nothing from God. As there is no mercy too great for God to give, so there is no mercy too little for us to ask. Certainly the man who thinks any mercy not worth seeking has little worth in him.

==== 4. Labor to Have Your Blessings Blessed to You

Fourthly and lastly, I answer: every Christian should labor to enjoy his mercies in mercy. He should labor to have his blessings blessed to him; he should labor to have “the favor of Him who dwelt in the burning bush” with all he has (Deut 33:16; Gen 22:17). Now this is an everlasting truth, a maxim to live and die with: whatever mercy does not come in on the wing of prayer is not given in mercy. Oh, how sweet is the mercy that comes flying in on the wing of prayer! How sweet to Samson was the water which streamed to him in the channel of private prayer (Judg 15:19); he called it En-hakkore, the well of him who prayed. Samson prayed as if for his life, and the water that was handed to him was as sweet as life. Every mercy that is gathered by the hand of prayer is as sweet as the rose of Sharon (Song 2:1). But the mercy that does not come in at the door of prayer does not come in at the right door; and the mercy that does not come in at the right door will do a man no good. Such mercies will make themselves wings and fly from us (Prov 23:5).

Every Christian should look closely to see that all his mercies are sanctified mercies. Now, every mercy is sanctified by the Word and prayer (1 Tim 4:4–5). Prayer prepares and fits us for mercy, and mercy for us. It is prayer that gives us a right and holy use of all our mercies. Mercies that do not come in on the wing of prayer are only great miseries. Prayerless men’s mercies are all given in wrath; indeed, their blessings are cursed to them (Prov 3:33; Mal 2:2). Just as every sacrifice was to be seasoned with salt, so every mercy is to be sanctified by prayer. Just as gold is sometimes laid not only on cloth and silks but also on silver itself, so prayer is the golden duty that must be laid not only on all our natural and civil actions, such as eating, drinking, buying, selling, etc., but also on all our silver duties, on all our most religious and spiritual exercises, such as hearing, reading, meditating, spiritual conversation, church fellowship, breaking of bread, etc.

Certainly, prayer is very necessary to make every providence, and every ordinance, and every mercy a blessing to us. Every mercy that comes in on the wing of private prayer is a double mercy. It is a pregnant mercy; it is a mercy that has many mercies in its womb. Happy is the Christian who can lay his hand on every mercy that he enjoys and say of them all, as Hannah once said of her Samuel, “I prayed for this boy, and the #smallcaps[Lord] has granted me what I asked of Him” (1 Sam 1:27).

=== Objection 5: I Lack a Convenient Place to Retire Into

Fifthly, some may further object and say, “I would carry on a private trade with God, I would exercise myself in secret prayer, but I lack a convenient place to retire into. I lack a private corner in which to unburden my soul to my Father.”

To this objection, I shall give these three short answers:

==== 1. This Objection Concerns but a Few Christians in Our Days

First, I suppose this objection concerns only a few Christians in our days. The God who has given a Christ to believers commonly gives them a convenient corner in which to enjoy private communion with Himself (Rom 8:32). Most Christians, I am afraid, lack a heart for private prayer rather than a convenient place for private prayer. What men set their hearts on, they will find time and place to carry out, whether it is good or evil, whether it concerns temporal or spiritual things, whether it concerns this world or another world, this life or a better life. If most men would only get better hearts, they would quickly find or make convenient places for private prayer. He who has an inflamed love for God will certainly find a corner in which to enjoy secret communion with God. True lovers will find corners in which to enjoy one another. How many men there are who can easily find private places for their dogs to lie in, and their swine to sleep in, and their horses to stand in, and their oxen to feed in, who cannot find a private place to seek the face of God in! But if these men only loved their God, or their souls, or private prayer, or eternity, as well as or better than their beasts, they would not be such brutes as not to find quickly a hole, a corner, to wait on the Lord in.

==== 2. Peter on the House-Top, Isaac in the Field, Christ on the Mountain

Secondly, I answer: if a Christian is on the top of a house with Peter, he may pray there; or if he is walking in the field with Isaac, he may pray there; or if he is on the mountain with Christ, he may pray there; or if he is behind the door with Paul, he may pray there; or if he is waiting at table with Nehemiah, he may secretly pray there; or if he is in a wood, he may pray there, as the early Christians did in times of persecution; or if he is behind a tree, he may pray there; or if he is by the seaside, he may pray there, as the apostles did. It was a choice saying of Augustine: “Every saint is God’s temple,” he says, “and he who carries his temple about with him may go to prayer when he pleases.” Some saints have never had so much of heaven brought down into their hearts as when they have been with God in a corner. Oh, the secret manifestations of divine love, the secret kisses, the secret embraces, the secret influences, the secret communion with God, that many a precious Christian has had in the most solitary places! It may be behind the door, or behind the wall, or behind the hedge, or behind the arbor, or behind the tree, or behind the rock, or behind the bush.

==== 3. You Once Found Secret Corners Enough to Sin In

Thirdly and lastly, did you never, in your unregenerate state, make use of all your wits, and talents, and utmost efforts, to find convenient times, and secret corners, and solitary places to sin in, and to dishonor your God in, and to ruin your own and others’ souls in? Yes! I remember with shame and blushing that it was so with me when I was dead in trespasses and sins and walked according to the course of this world (Eph 2:1–3). Oh, how much then does it concern you, in your renewed, sanctified, and raised state, to make use of all your wits, and talents, and utmost efforts to find the fittest times, and the most secret corners, and the most solitary places you can, to honor your God in, and to seek the welfare of your own and others’ souls in! Oh, that men were only as serious, studious, and industrious to find convenient times and secret places to please and serve and glorify the Lord in as they have been serious, studious, and industrious to find convenient times and secret places to displease and grieve the Spirit of the Lord in!

=== Objection 6: Our Infirmities and Weaknesses Shut Us Up

Sixthly, and lastly, others may further object and say, We would be often in private with God; we would give ourselves up to closet prayer. But we can no sooner shut our closet doors than a multitude of infirmities, weaknesses, and vanities face us and rise up against us. Our hearts are full of disorders and follies. Our bodies, say some, are under great indispositions; and our souls, say others, are under present indispositions. How then can we seek the face of God in a corner? How can we wrestle with God in our closets?

Now, to this objection I shall give these six answers.

==== 1. By This Reasoning, Where Lives the Man or Woman That Could Pray?

If these kinds of reasonings or arguments were enough to shut private prayer out of doors, where lives that man or woman, that husband or wife, that father or child, that master or servant, who would ever be found in the practice of that duty? Where is there a person under heaven whose heart is not full of infirmities, weaknesses, follies, and vanities, and whose body and soul are not too often indisposed to closet duties? “When they sin against You—for there is no one who does not sin—” (1 Kgs 8:46). “Surely there is no righteous man on earth who does good and never sins” (Eccl 7:20). “Who can say, ‘I have kept my heart pure; I am cleansed from my sin’?” (Prov 20:9). “Who can bring out clean from unclean? No one!” (Job 14:4). “If I should wash myself with snow and cleanse my hands with lye, then You would plunge me into the pit, and even my own clothes would despise me” (Job 9:30–31). “Even if I were righteous, my mouth would condemn me; if I were perfect, it would declare me guilty” (Job 9:20). “Do not bring Your servant into judgment, for no one alive is righteous before You” (Ps 143:2). “We all stumble in many ways” (Jas 3:2). “If we say we have no sin, we deceive ourselves, and the truth is not in us” (1 John 1:8).

Those who affirm that men may be fully perfect in this life, or without sin in this life, affirm what is expressly contrary to the Scriptures just cited, and to the universal experience of all saints, who daily feel and lament over that body of sin and death that they carry about with them. Indeed, they affirm what is quite contrary to the very state or constitution of all the saints in this life. In every saint “the flesh craves what is contrary to the Spirit, and the Spirit what is contrary to the flesh. They are opposed to each other, so that you#super[+] cannot do what you want” (Gal 5:17). In every good man there are two men, the old man and the new. The new must be put on daily, and the old put off daily (Eph 4:22–24). All saints have a law in their members rebelling against the law of their minds, so that the good that they would do, they do not, and the evil that they would not do, that they do (Rom 7:23, 25). They have two contrary principles in them, from which come two kinds of actions, stirrings, and inclinations, continually opposed to one another. That is why there is a continual combat in them, like the struggling of the twins in Rebekah’s womb.

An absolute perfection belongs only to the triumphant state of God’s elect in heaven. Heaven is the only privileged place where no unclean thing can enter (Rev 21:27); it is the only place where neither sin nor Satan will ever get a footing. Those who dream of an absolute perfection in this life confuse and jumble heaven and earth together—the state of the church militant with the state of the church triumphant, which are certainly distinct both in time and place, and in order, measure, and accompaniments (Heb 12:22–23).

This dangerous opinion of absolute perfection in this life shakes the very foundation of religion and overthrows the gospel of grace. It renders the satisfaction of Christ and all His great transactions null and void. It tells the world that there is no need of faith, of repentance, of ordinances, of watchfulness. Those who say they have no sin say they have no need of the blood of Christ to cleanse them from sin (1 John 1:7). Those who say they have no sin say they have no need of faith to rest on Christ for imputed righteousness to justify their persons. Those who say they have no sin say they have no need of Christ as king to subdue their lusts, nor as a priest to atone for offenses, nor as prophet to teach and instruct them, nor as a Savior to save them from their sins or from the wrath to come (Matt 1:21; 1 Thess 1:10). Those who have a perfect righteousness of their own need not be indebted to Christ for His pure, perfect, spotless, matchless righteousness. Those who are without sin have no cause to repent of sin, nor yet to watch against sin. Those who are perfect cannot say, We are unprofitable servants. But are they indeed just? Then they must live by faith (Hab 2:4). Are they men, and not angels? Then they must repent. “He now commands all men everywhere to repent” (Acts 17:30). Surely the best of men are only men at best. Oh, how bad those men must be who make God Himself a liar (1 John 1:10). But if these men are absolutely perfect, how is it that they are afflicted and diseased like other men? How is it that they eat, and drink, and sleep, and buy, and sell, and die like other men? Are these things consistent with an absolute perfection? Surely not. An absolute perfection is not a step short of heaven; it is heaven on this side of heaven, and those who would obtain it must step into heaven before they have it.

==== 2. This Objection Lies Against All Other Kinds of Prayer

Secondly, I answer that this objection lies as strongly against family prayer, and against every other kind of prayer, as it does against closet prayer. He who on any grounds makes this objection a great bugbear to scare his soul from closet prayer may on the same grounds make it a great bugbear to scare his soul not only from every other kind of prayer, but from all other duties of religion as well, whether private or public. The spirit of this objection fights against all religion at once. Therefore you should say to it, as Christ said to Peter, “Get behind Me, Satan!” (Matt 16:23).

==== 3. Infirmities Seen, Lamented, and Resisted Cannot Obstruct Prayer

Thirdly, I answer, It is not the infirmities and weaknesses of a Christian that are seen, lamented, bewailed, and resisted that can obstruct or hinder the efficacy and success of his prayers. Let me make this clear in a few instances. Jonah, you know, was a man full of sinful passions and other weaknesses, and yet his prayer prevailed greatly with God (Jonah 2:1–2, 7, 10).

So Elijah’s prayers prevailed exceedingly with God. He could open and shut heaven at his pleasure, and yet he was “subject to the same passions as we are” (Jas 5:17). Elijah was a man of extraordinary sanctity and holiness, a man who lived in heaven while he dwelt on earth. Like Enoch, he walked with God, and yet he was “subject to the same passions as we are” (1 Kgs 19:8; Rom 11:2–3). God in an eminent way imparted to him His counsel and secrets. He lay in the bosom of the Father, and yet he “was a man subject to the same passions as we are.” He was a very powerful and prevailing prophet. His very name means as much: Elijah signifies “my strong God.” In 1 Kings 17:1 it is #emph[Eli-jahu], that is, the Lord, He is my strong God; and yet he was “subject to the same passions as we are.” He was a man much in fasting and prayer; he was an inferior mediator between God and His people; and yet he was “subject to the same passions as we are.”

Now some might object from this and say, “No wonder such a man as he was could open and shut heaven at his pleasure by his prayers. But I am a poor, weak, low, sinful, and unworthy creature. I am full of infirmities, weaknesses, and passions. Will my prayers ever find access to God and acceptance with God, or gracious answers and replies from God?” Now to answer this objection, and to remove this discouragement from the thoughts and hearts of poor sinners, the Holy Spirit adds this clause: that he was not a god, nor an angel, but a man, and such a man as was not exempt from common infirmities, for he had his passions, frailties, and weaknesses as well as other saints. This shows us that infirmities in the lowliest saints should no more harm the acceptance and success of their prayers with God than they did in Elijah himself.

The word #emph[passion] sometimes signifies, first, a stirring of the sensual appetite, arising from the imagination of good or ill, with some commotion of the body. Secondly, #emph[passions] sometimes signify sinful infirmities, sinful disturbances of the mind. And thirdly, #emph[passion] is sometimes taken more strictly for the particular emotion of sinful anger and wrath, which Chrysostom calls #emph[brevis daemon], a short devil. It makes a man say he knows not what, as you may see in Jonah, and do he knows not what, as you may see in Saul. Now in these last two senses, Elijah was a man subject to the same passions as we are, and yet a man so mighty with God that by private prayer he could do even what he pleased in the court of heaven.

In 1 Samuel 21 you may read of David’s outright lies, and of his other failings, infirmities, and unseemly behavior before Achish, king of Gath, for which he was turned out of the king’s presence as a madman. And yet at that very time he prays and prevails with God for favor, mercy, and deliverance. “I sought the #smallcaps[Lord], and He answered me; He delivered me from all my fears” (Ps 34:4). But when was this? Read the title of the psalm, and you will find it: “Of David, when he pretended to be insane before Abimelech, who drove him away, and he departed.”

In Numbers 20:10–12, Moses’ infirmities are pointed out. First, you have there his immoderate anger. (2) His speaking to the people when he should have spoken to the rock (v. 8). (3) His striking it when he should only have spoken to it with the rod in his hand, and striking it twice, as in a fit of passion and impatience. (4) His distrust of the Lord’s word (v. 12). (5) His reviling the people when he should have convicted them: “Listen now, you#super[+] rebels” (v. 10). (6) He seems so offended at his commission that he can hardly keep from grumbling: “Must we bring…water out of this rock?” (v. 10). Mark that word, “must we.” Oh, how the meekest man in all the world (Num 12:3) is carried away into passion, and anger, and unbelief, and hurried into sad improprieties! And yet there was not a man on earth whose prayers were so powerful and prevailing with God as Moses’ were (Ps 106:23; Exod 32:9–15; 33:11–17; 14:13–16).

So King Asa was a man full of infirmities and weaknesses. He relies on the king of Syria and not on the Lord (2 Chr 16:7–13). He is very impatient and in a great rage at the seer’s rebuke. He imprisons the seer. He oppressed some of the people at the same time; or, as the Hebrew has it, “he crushed,” or trampled on, some of the people. And being greatly diseased in his feet, he sought the physicians and not the Lord. And yet this man’s prayer prevailed wonderfully with God (2 Chr 14:11–15).

The saints’ infirmities can never make void those gracious promises by which God stands bound to listen to the prayers of His people (Ps 50:15; Isa 30:19; 65:24). God’s hearing of our prayers does not depend on sanctification, but on Christ’s intercession; not on what we are in ourselves, but on what we are in the Lord Jesus. Both our persons and our prayers are acceptable in the beloved (Eph 1:6; 1 Pet 2:5). When God hears our prayers, it is neither for our own sakes nor yet for our prayers’ sake, but for His own sake, and His Son’s sake, and His glory’s sake, and His promise’s sake.

Certainly, God will never cast off His people for their infirmities.

First, it is the glory of a man to pass by infirmities (Prov 19:11). Oh, how much more, then, must it be the glory of God to pass by the infirmities of His people!

Secondly, saints are children; and what father will cast off his children for their infirmities and weaknesses (Ps 103:13–14)?

Thirdly, saints are members of Christ’s body (1 Cor 12:27); and what man will cut off a member because of a scab or wart on it? “What man will cut off his nose,” says Luther, “because there is some filth in it?”

Fourthly, saints are Christ’s purchase; they are His possession, His inheritance. Now what man is there who will cast away or cast off his purchase, his possession, his inheritance, because of thorns, bushes, or briars that grow on it?

Fifthly, saints are in a marriage covenant with God (Hos 2:19–20). Now what husband is there who will cast off his wife for her failings and infirmities? So long as a man is in covenant with God, his infirmities cannot cut him off from God’s mercy and grace. Now it is certain that a man may have very many infirmities on him, and yet not break his covenant with God; for no sin breaks a man’s covenant with God except one that unties the marriage knot. In other marriages, not every offense or infirmity annuls the marriage union; only the breach of the marriage vow, adultery, unties the marriage knot. So here, only those sins that break the covenant untie the marriage knot between God and the soul: (1) When men freely submit to any lust as a new master; or (2) When men take another husband. Men do this when they enter into a league with sin or the world, when they make a new covenant with hell and death (Isa 28:15, 18). Now God guards His chosen ones from these harms. In a word, if God cast off His people for their infirmities, then none of the sons or daughters of Adam could be saved. “Surely there is no righteous man on earth who does good and never sins” (Eccl 7:20). Now if God will not cast off His people for their infirmities, then certainly He will not cast off the prayers of His people because of those invincible infirmities that hang on them. Therefore our infirmities should not discourage us, or keep us from closet prayer, or from any other duties of religion.

==== 4. The More Infirmities Hang upon Us, the More Cause to Keep Close

Fourthly, I answer, The more infirmities and weaknesses hang on us, the more cause we have to keep close and constant to our closet duties. If grace is weak, the omission of private prayer will make it weaker. Just as he who will not eat will certainly grow weaker and weaker, so he who will not pray in his closet will certainly grow weaker and weaker. If corruptions are strong, the neglect of private prayer will make them stronger. The more the remedy is neglected, the more the disease is strengthened. Whatever the disorders of a man’s heart may be, they will never be lessened, but only increased, by the omission of private prayer. The more bodily infirmities hang on us, the more need we have of the physician. And so, the more sinful infirmities hang on our souls, the more need we have of private prayer. All sinful omissions will make work for repentance, for hell, or for the physician of souls. Sinful omissions lead to sinful commissions, as you may see in the angels who fell from heaven to hell, and in Adam’s fall in paradise.

Origen, going to comfort and encourage a martyr who was to be tortured, was himself arrested by the officers and forced either to offer to the idols or to have his body abused by a Moor who was ready for that purpose. Given that hard choice, to save his life he bowed to the idol. But afterward, making a sad confession of his foul deed, he said that he had gone out that morning before he had been with God in his closet; and so he firmly concluded that his neglect of prayer was the cause of his falling into that great sin.

The neglect of one day, of one duty, of one hour, would undo us forever if we did not have “an advocate with the Father” (1 John 2:1–2). Those years, those months, those weeks, those days, those hours that are not filled up with God, with Christ, with grace, with duty, will certainly be filled up with vanity and folly. All omissions of duty will more and more unfit the soul for duty. A key thrown aside gathers rust; a pump not used is hard to get going; and armor not used is hard to make bright. Just as sinful commissions will stab the soul, so sinful omissions will starve the soul. Those who live in the neglect of private prayer may well cry out, “Our leanness, our leanness!” (Isa 24:16; Job 16:8). Therefore away with all these pleas and reasonings about infirmities, and weaknesses, and indispositions, and give yourselves#super[+] to closet prayer.

==== 5. Your Illness Does Not Hinder Your Buying, Selling, and Getting Gain

Fifthly, I answer, It may be that your illness and indisposition of body is not so great that you cannot buy, sell, and get gain. In spite of your aching head, and the shooting pains in your back, and your painful sides, and your feeble knees, you can still, like Martha, busy yourself with your worldly affairs.

In Song of Solomon 5:2, Christ calls on His spouse to open the door and let Him in. But sin and excuse-making came into the world together; and see how poorly and unworthily she tries to put Christ off: “I have taken off my robe—must I put it back on? I have washed my feet—must I soil them again?” (v. 3). Rather than make no excuse for herself, she will make a foolish excuse, a worthless excuse. She was no longer a child, and what great trouble would it have been for her to rise and let in such a guest, who brings with Him everything the heart can wish or need can require (Rev 3:17–18)? She had not grown so frail with old age that she was unable to get herself ready. At the least, she might easily have slipped on her morning gown and stepped to the door without any danger of catching cold or of being soaked to the skin, and so have let in Him who never comes empty-handed (Rev 22:12)—indeed, Who had now come full of the dew of divine blessings to enrich her, for so some interpret those words, “My head is drenched with dew, my hair with the dampness of the night” (Song 5:2). Oh, the frivolous pretenses and idle excuses that even gracious persons are apt at times to take up to gloss over their neglect of duty!

But some may say, It may be that the spouse of Christ was asleep. Oh, no! For she says, “I sleep, but my heart is awake” (v. 2). She slept with open eyes, as the lion does. She was only half asleep. Though her outward man was drowsy, her inward man was wakeful. Though the flesh took a nap, her spirit did not nod.

Oh, but it may be that Christ made no noise. He gave no notice that He was at the door. Oh, yes! He knocked; He knocked and pounded with the hammer of His Word and the hand of His Spirit. He knocked by outward corrections and inward admonitions. He knocked by providences, and He knocked by mercies. His importunity and urgency to be let in were very great.

Oh, but it may be that He only knocked. He should have called as well as knocked; for none but madmen would open their doors in the night unless they knew the voice of Him who knocks. Oh, yes! He did not only knock but called also.

Oh, but it may be that she did not know His voice, and therefore she would not open. No chaste wife will rise at unseasonable hours and open her doors to a stranger, especially in her husband’s absence. Oh, yes, she knew His voice: “The voice of my beloved! He is knocking” (v. 2). She was not so fast asleep that she did not know the voice of her beloved from all other voices, and could not tell every word that He said. The calls of Christ were so strong, so loud, and His knocks so mighty, that she could not help but know and confess that it was the voice of her beloved, though she was not so respectful and dutiful as to obey that voice.

Oh, but it may be that Christ knocked and called like a friend on his journey, only to ask how it was with her, or to speak to her at the window. Oh, no! He speaks plainly; He speaks with authority: “Open to me” (v. 2).

Oh, but it may be that she had no power to open the door. Oh, yes, she had; for when He commands His people to open, He lends them a key to open the door so that He may enter (Phil 1:6; 2:13; 1 Cor 15:10). Infused grace is a living principle that will enable the soul to open to Christ. If a man is not a free agent to work and act by the helps of grace received, to what purpose are counsels, commands, exhortations, and directions given to perform this, and that, and the other work? And certainly it is our greatest honor and happiness in this world to cooperate with God in those things that concern His own glory and our own internal and eternal good.

Oh, but it may be that Christ had given His spouse some offense, or had let fall some hard words or unkind speeches, which made her a little perverse and peevish. Oh, no! For He acknowledges her as His beloved, and courts her highly with the most winning and friendly terms of love: “My sister, my darling, my dove, my undefiled,” or “my flawless one.” He calls her so for her dovelike simplicity, purity, and integrity. All these endearing and honoring titles are the rhetoric of divine love, and should have been so many sacred bonds on her to open to her beloved.

Oh, but it may be that Christ was too quick for her; it may be that He gave only a knock and a call and was gone before she could rise and open the door. Oh, no! Christ stayed until His head was drenched with dew, and His hair with the dampness of the night. This most passionate expression shows the tender goodness, patience, and gentleness of our Lord Jesus, Who endures far greater and harder things for His spouse’s sake than Jacob ever did for his Rachel’s sake. After Christ had suffered much for her sake and waited a long while for her to be ready, she very unkindly, very rudely, and unworthily turns her back on all His sweet and comforting names and His blessed and bleeding embraces, and sends Him away to look \[for\] His lodging in some other place. So He might well have said, Is this your kindness to your friend, your husband, your Lord, to let Him stand bareheaded, and that in foul weather, indeed in the nighttime, wooing, entreating, and begging to be let in, and yet to send Him away as one in Whom your soul could take no pleasure?

Now, if you will only seriously weigh all these circumstances in the balance of the sanctuary, you may read at a run the fault and folly, the weakness and madness, the carelessness and laziness of the spouse. And by her you may judge of those sad and sinful disorders that may seize on the best of saints, and see how ready the flesh is to frame excuses, and all to keep the soul from duty and the doors bolted fast against the Lord Jesus.

It is sad when men are well enough to sit, chat, and trade in their shops, but not well enough to pray in their closets. Certainly that man’s heart is not right with God, at least at this time, who under all his bodily ailments can maintain and keep up his public trade with men, but is not well enough to maintain his private trade with heaven.

Our bodies are only dirt, handsomely tempered and skillfully formed; we derive our pedigree from the dirt and are akin to clay. One calls the body “the blot of nature”; another calls it “the soul’s beast,” “a sack of dung,” “worms’ food”; another calls it “a prison,” “a tomb”; and Paul calls it “a body of vileness.” Now for a man to make so much fuss about the ailments of his body to excuse the neglect of his soul is an evil made up of many evils. But really, sir, I am so ill, and my body is so ailing and indisposed, that I am not able to attend to or deal with the least things of the world! Well! If this is so, then know that God has knocked you off from the things of this world on purpose, so that you may look more effectually after the things of another world. God’s design in all the ailments that are on your body is to wind you further off from your worldly trade, and to bring you to follow your heavenly trade more closely. Many a man would never have found the way to his closet if God had not turned him out of his shop, his trade, his business, his all, etc., by bodily ailments.

Well, Christians, remember this once for all: if your#super[+] indisposition to closet prayer really arises from bodily ailments, then you may be confident that the Lord will pity you much, and bear with you much, and kindly accept a little. You#super[+] know how affectionately parents and noble-minded masters behave toward their children and servants when they are under bodily ailments and indisposition; and you may be confident that God will never deal worse with you than they do with them. Ponder Ezek 34:4, 16, 21–22 often.

==== 6. A Distinction: Contracted and Involuntary Indisposition

Sixthly, and lastly, I shall answer this objection by way of distinction, as follows:

First, there is a contracted indisposition to private prayer, and there is an involuntary indisposition to private prayer. There is a contracted indisposition, and that is when a man, by his willful sinning against light, knowledge, conviction, etc., contracts guilt that lies as a load on his conscience. Now guilt makes the soul shy of God; and the greater the guilt is, the more shy the soul is of drawing near to God in a corner. The child who feels himself under guilt hides himself from his father’s eye in the day, as Adam did, and at night slips off to bed to avoid either a scolding or a whipping from his father (Gen 3:7–8). Guilt makes a man flee from God and flee from prayer. It is a hard thing to look God in the face when guilt stares a man in the face (Job 11:14–15). Guilt makes a man a terror to himself (Jer 20:3–4). Now when a man is a terror to himself, he is neither fit to live, nor fit to die, nor fit to pray. When poison gets into the body, it works on the spirits; it weakens the spirits, endangers life, and unfits and indisposes a man for all natural actions. So it is here. When guilt lies heavy on the conscience, it works on the soul, it weakens the soul, it endangers the soul, and it greatly unfits and indisposes the soul for all holy actions. Guilt fights against our souls, our consciences, our comforts, our duties, and indeed our very graces also (1 Pet 2:11).

There is nothing that wounds and lames our graces like guilt. There is nothing that weakens and wastes our graces like guilt. There is nothing that hinders the activity of our graces like guilt; nor is there anything that clouds our evidences of grace like guilt. What water is to fire, our sins are to our graces, evidences, and duties. Guilt is like Prometheus’ vulture, which always lies gnawing. It is better, with Evagrius, to lie on a bed of straw with a good conscience than to lie on a bed of down with a guilty conscience. What the would-be disciple said to our Savior—“Teacher, I will follow You wherever You go” (Matt 8:19)—a guilty conscience says to the sinner: “Wherever you go, I will follow you. If you go to a fast, I will follow you, and fill your mind with black and dismal thoughts of God. If you go to a feast, I will follow you, and show you the handwriting on the wall (Dan 5:5). If you go away from home, I will follow you, and make you afraid of every leaf that stirs. You will look on every bush as an armed man, and on every man as a devil. If you stay at home, I will follow you from room to room, and fill you with horror and terror. If you lie down to rest, I will follow you with fearful dreams and tormenting apparitions. If you go into your closet, I will follow you, and make your very closet a hell to hold you.”

It is told of King Richard the Third that after he had murdered his two nephews in the Tower, guilt lay so hard on his conscience that his sleep was very unquiet. He would often leap out of his bed in the dark, catch up in his hand his sword, which hung by his bedside, and go distractedly about his room seeking the traitor.

So Charles the Ninth of France, after he had made the streets of Paris run with the blood of the Protestants, could seldom get any sound sleep, nor could he bear to be awakened out of his sleep without music.

Judge Morgan, who passed the sentence of condemnation on Jane Grey, a virtuous lady, shortly afterward went mad, and in his raving cried out continually, “Take away the Lady Jane from me, take away the Lady Jane from me,” and in that horror ended his wretched life.

James Abyes, going to execution for Christ’s sake, gave his money and his clothes to one and another as he went along, until he had given everything away down to his shirt. At this one of the sheriff’s men began to scoff at him and deride him, and told him that he was a madman and a heretic and not to be believed. But as soon as the good man was executed, this wretch was struck mad, threw away his clothes, and cried out that James Abyes was a good man and gone to heaven, but that he himself was a wicked man and was damned; and he went on crying out like this until his death. Certainly, if a man derides or strikes another for walking according to the Word of the Lord, the Lord will, sooner or later, so strike and wound the scoffer’s conscience that all the physicians in the world will not heal it.

Now if your indisposition to private prayer springs from guilt contracted on your conscience, then your best way is to renew your repentance speedily, to judge and humble your own soul greatly, and so to exercise faith afresh on the blood of Christ, both for pardoning mercy and for purging grace. When a man is stung with guilt, it is his highest wisdom in the world to look up to the bronze serpent, and not to spend his time or create torments for his own soul by endlessly poring over his guilt. When guilt on the conscience brings a man to water the earth with tears and to make heaven ring with his groans, then it works as it should. When the sense of guilt drives a man to God, to duty, to the throne of grace, then it will not be night long for that man. He who thinks to evade private prayer under the pretense of guilt only increases his own guilt by it. Neglect of duty will never get guilt off the conscience.

But then there is an #emph[involuntary] indisposition to private prayer, as in a sick man who would work and walk but cannot, being hindered by his disease; or as with a man who has a great chain on his leg: he would very gladly walk or get away, but his chain hinders him. Now if your#super[+] indisposition to private prayer is an involuntary indisposition, then God will in mercy, in due course, both pardon it and remove it.

Secondly, there is a #emph[total] indisposition to private prayer, and there is a #emph[partial] indisposition to private prayer. A total indisposition to private prayer is when a man has no mind at all for private prayer, nor any will at all for private prayer, nor any love at all for private prayer, nor any delight, nor any heart at all for private prayer (Jer 4:22; 44:17–19). Now where this frame of heart is, there all is wicked, very wicked, utterly wicked. A partial indisposition to private prayer is when a man has some will for private prayer, though not such a will as he once had; and some mind for private prayer, though not such a mind as he once had; and some affection for private prayer, though not such warm and burning affections as he once had. Now if your#super[+] indisposition to private prayer is total, then you must wait on the Lord in all His appointed means for a changed nature and for union with Christ. But if your#super[+] indisposition to private prayer is only partial, then the Lord will certainly pardon it, and in time remove it in the very use of holy means.

Thirdly and lastly, there is a #emph[transient], accidental, occasional, or fleeting indisposition to private prayer; and there is a #emph[customary], a constant, or permanent indisposition to private prayer.

Now a transient, accidental, occasional, or fleeting indisposition to what is good may be found on the best of saints, as you may see in Moses (Exod 4:10–14), and in Jeremiah (Jer 1:5–8, 17–19; 20:9), and in Jonah (Jon 1), and in David (Ps 39:2–3). Now if this is the indisposition you are under, then you may be confident that it will certainly wear off by degrees, as theirs did whom I have just cited.

But then there is a customary, a constant, or permanent indisposition to private prayer, and to all other holy duties of religion (Isa 65:2). Now if this is the indisposition you are under, then I may safely conclude that you are “poisoned by bitterness and captive to iniquity” in the extreme (Acts 8:21–23). And your work lies not in complaining of your indisposition but in repenting and believing, and in laboring for a change of your heart and state; for until your heart and your state are changed, you will remain forever indisposed both to closet prayer and to all other duties of religion and godliness. To see a sinner sailing hellward with wind and tide on his side alter his course and tack about for heaven; to see the earthly man become heavenly, the carnal man become spiritual, the proud man become humble, the frivolous man become serious; to see a sinner move contrary to himself in the ways of Christ and holiness, is as strange as to see the earth fly upward, or the bowl run against its own bias. And yet a divine power of God on the soul can bring it about; and this must be brought about before the sinner will be graciously inclined and sincerely disposed to closet prayer. And let this much suffice by way of answer to this objection also.
